Only three of the thirteen B-52As ordered were built.{{sfn|Tagg|2004|p=85}} All were returned to Boeing and used in their test program.{{Sfn|Knaack|1988|p=230}} On 9 June 1952, the February 1951 contract was updated to order the aircraft under new specifications. The final 10, the first aircraft to enter active service, were completed as B-52Bs.{{Sfn|Knaack|1988|p=230}} At the roll-out ceremony on 18 March 1954, Air Force Chief of Staff General [[Nathan Farragut Twining|Nathan Twining]] said:
{{Blockquote | style=font-size:100% | The [[long rifle]] was the great weapon of its day.{{nbsp}}... today this B-52 is the long rifle of the air age.{{sfnm|1a1=Knaack|1y=1988|1pp=229–230|2a12=Tagg|2y=2004|2p=5}}}}

The B-52B was followed by progressively improved bomber and reconnaissance variants, culminating in the B-52G and [[turbofan]] B-52H. To allow rapid delivery, production lines were set up both at its main Seattle factory and at Boeing's [[Wichita, Kansas|Wichita]] facility. More than 5,000 companies were involved in the huge production effort, with 41% of the airframe being built by subcontractors.{{sfn|Gunston|1957|p=776}} The prototypes and all B-52A, B and C models (90 aircraft){{sfn|Lake|2003a|pp=117–121}} were built at Seattle. Testing of aircraft built in Seattle caused problems due to jet noise, which led to the establishment of curfews for engine tests. Aircraft were ferried {{convert|150|mi}} east on their maiden flights to [[Larson Air Force Base]] near [[Moses Lake, Washington|Moses Lake]], where they were fully tested.{{sfn|Bowers|1989|p=379}}

As production of the B-47 came to an end, the Wichita factory was phased in for B-52D production, with Seattle responsible for 101 D-models and Wichita 69.{{sfn|Lake|2003b|pp=100–101}} Both plants continued to build the B-52E, with 42 built at Seattle and 58 at Wichita,{{sfn|Lake|2003b|p=102}} and the B-52F (44 from Seattle and 45 from Wichita).{{sfn|Lake|2003b|p=103}} For the B-52G, Boeing decided in 1957 to transfer all production to Wichita, which freed up Seattle for other tasks, particularly the production of airliners.{{sfnm|1a1=Gunston|1y=1957|1p=778|2a1=Lake|2y=2003b|2pp=101}} Production ended in 1962 with the B-52H, with 742 aircraft built, plus the original two prototypes.{{sfn|Eden|2004|p=71}}

===<span class="anchor" id="Upgrades and modifications"></span>Upgrades===
A proposed variant of the B-52H was the EB-52H, which would have consisted of 16 modified and augmented B-52H airframes with additional [[Electronic warfare|electronic jamming]] capabilities.{{sfnm|1a1=Tagg|1y=2004|1p=91|2a1=Trimble|2y=2009}}<ref>{{cite web |title=USAF Radar Jamming Technology |url=http://proxify.org/p/001010A1000000/687474703a2f2f7777772e61726d6564666f726365732d696e742e636f6d2f6e6577732f757361665f72616461725f6a616d6d696e675f746563686e6f6c6f67792e68746d6c |website=Proxify.org |access-date=9 April 2010}}{{Better source needed|reason=The current source is insufficiently reliable ([[WP:NOTRS]]).|date=March 2023}}</ref> This variant would have restored USAF airborne jamming capability that it lost on retiring the [[EF-111 Raven]]. The program was canceled in 2005 following the removal of funds for the [[stand-off jammer]]. The program was revived in 2007 and cut again in early 2009.{{sfn|Trimble|2009}}

In July 2013, the USAF began a fleet-wide technological upgrade of its B-52 bombers called Combat Network Communications Technology (CONECT) to modernize electronics, communications technology, computing, and avionics on the flight deck. CONECT upgrades include software and hardware such as new computer servers, modems, radios, data-links, receivers, and digital workstations for the crew. One update is the [[AN/ARC-210]] Warrior beyond-line-of-sight software programmable radio able to transmit voice, data, and information in-flight between B-52s and ground command and control centers, allowing the transmission and reception of data with updated intelligence, mapping, and targeting information; previous in-flight target changes required copying down coordinates. The ARC-210 allows machine-to-machine transfer of data, useful on long-endurance missions where targets may have moved before the arrival of the B-52. The aircraft will be able to receive information through [[Link-16]]. CONECT upgrades will cost {{US$|1.1}}{{nbsp}}billion overall and take several years. Funding has been secured for 30 B-52s; the USAF hopes for 10 CONECT upgrades per year, but the rate has yet to be decided.<ref name="CONECT">{{cite web |title=Air Force Begins Massive B-52 Overhaul |url=http://www.dodbuzz.com/2013/07/12/air-force-begins-massive-b-52-overhaul/ |website=DoDBuzz.com |date=12 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171205154135/https://www.dodbuzz.com/2013/07/12/air-force-begins-massive-b-52-overhaul/ |archive-date=5 December 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |author=Defense Industry Daily staff |title=CONECT: B-52H Reach Digital Age with Communications Upgrades |url=http://www.defenseindustrydaily.com/b52h-receiving-216m-communications-upgrade-0278/ |date=12 May 2014 |access-date=1 March 2023 |website=Defense Industry Daily}}</ref>

Weapons upgrades include the 1760 Internal Weapons Bay Upgrade (IWBU), which gives a 66 percent increase in weapons payload using a digital interface ([[MIL-STD-1760]]) and rotary launcher. IWBU is expected to cost roughly {{US$|313}}{{nbsp}}million.<ref name="CONECT"/> The 1760 IWBU will allow the B-52 to carry eight<ref>{{cite web |title=Meet the 'New' B-52 Bomber: How This Old Plane Can Drop Even More Bombs |first=Kris |last=Osborn |date=6 March 2018 |website=[[The National Interest]] |url=https://nationalinterest.org/blog/the-buzz/meet-the-new-b-52-bomber-how-old-plane-can-drop-even-more-24768}}</ref> [[JDAM]] {{convert|2000|lb|adj=on}} bombs, [[AGM-158 JASSM#Improved JASSM versions|AGM-158B JASSM-ER]] cruise missiles and [[ADM-160 MALD|ADM-160C MALD-J]] decoy missiles internally. All 1760 IWBUs should be operational by October 2017. Two bombers will have the ability to carry 40 weapons in place of the 36 that three B-52s can carry.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.deagel.com/news/n000011943 |title=USAF B-52 Bomber Internal Weapons Bay to Increase its Capability |website=Deagel.com |date=23 September 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221211194420/https://www.deagel.com/news/n000011943 |archive-date=11 December 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> The 1760 IWBU allows precision-guided missiles or bombs to be deployed from inside the weapons bay; the previous aircraft carried these munitions externally on the wing [[hardpoint]]s. This increases the number of guided weapons ([[Joint Direct Attack Munition]] or JDAM) a B-52 can carry and reduces the need for guided bombs to be carried on the wings. The first phase will allow a B-52 to carry twenty-four GBU-38 500-pound guided bombs or twenty GBU-31 2,000-pound bombs, with later phases accommodating the JASSM and MALD family of missiles.<ref>{{cite news |last=Ray |first=Mike W. |title=B-52 upgrade to increase smart-weapons capacity |publisher=[[United States Air Force]] |date=8 January 2014 |url=https://www.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/467901/b-52-upgrade-to-increase-smart-weapons-capacity/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230208162643/https://www.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/467901/b-52-upgrade-to-increase-smart-weapons-capacity/ |archive-date=8 February 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> In addition to carrying more smart bombs, moving them internally from the wings reduces drag and achieves a 15 percent reduction in fuel consumption.<ref>{{cite web |last=Szondy |first=David |url=https://newatlas.com/b-52-weapons-bay/35706/ |title=The B-52 gets upgraded weapons bay |work=New Atlas |date=23 January 2015}}</ref>

In 2017, the US Air Force Research Lab was investigating defensive laser weapons for the B-52.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://nationalinterest.org/blog/the-buzz/us-air-force-scientists-are-working-arm-the-b-52-bomber-19360 |title=U.S. Air Force Scientists are Working to Arm the B-52 Bomber with Laser Weapons |first=Kris |last=Osborn |date=7 February 2017 |work=[[The National Interest]]}}</ref>

The US Air Force extended a 2020 solicitation called the '''B-52 Commercial Engine Replacement Program'''<!-- bolded per [[WP:MOSBOLD]] as a redirect target -->. Both General Electric and [[Rolls-Royce North America]] submitted proposals; GE with [[General Electric CF34|CF34-10]] engines<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.geaviation.com/military/engines/b-52|access-date= 25 June 2020|title= Propulsion Hub & Engine Product &#124; GE Aviation|archive-date= 26 June 2020|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200626043209/https://www.geaviation.com/military/engines/b-52|url-status= dead}}</ref> and Rolls-Royce with the [[Rolls-Royce F130]]. In September 2021, the F130 was selected.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.rolls-royce.com/media/press-releases/2021/24-09-2021-rr-north-america-selected-to-power-the-b-52-commercial-engine-replacement-program.aspx|access-date= 21 July 2023|title= Rolls-Royce North America selected to power the B-52 Commercial Engine Replacement Program}}</ref>  The F130 has {{convert|17000|lbf|kN|abbr=on|sigfig=3}} thrust, similar to the existing B-52 engines ([[Pratt & Whitney JT3D|Pratt & Whitney TF33]]). The&nbsp;USAF intends to purchase 650 engines (608 direct replacements, 42 spares) for its fleet of 75 B-52H aircraft in a $2.6&nbsp;billion deal;<ref>{{Cite news |date=24 September 2021 |title=DAF awards Rolls-Royce B-52H engine contract |work=Department of the Air Force |url=https://www.hill.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/2788242/daf-awards-rolls-royce-b-52h-engine-contract/ |archive-date=17 May 2026 |access-date=5 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260517181917/https://www.hill.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/2788242/daf-awards-rolls-royce-b-52h-engine-contract/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> upgraded aircraft are redesignated B-52J.<ref name="Tirpak 2023">{{cite web|url=https://www.airandspaceforces.com/re-engined-b-52-b-52j/|title=It's Official: The Re-Engined B-52 Will be the B-52J|last=Tirpak|first=John A.|date=5 April 2023|work=Air & Space Forces Magazine|access-date=10 April 2023}}</ref>

In July 2026, a [[United States Government Accountability Office|US Government Accountability Office]] (GAO) report said that the "Commercial Engine Replacement Program" was running $3 billion over budget due to cost overruns and delays. The GAO criticised the Air Force for not using modern digital engineering methods to keep the program on track.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nationaldefensemagazine.org/articles/2026/7/31/b52-modernization-program-3-billion-over-budget |title=BREAKING: B-52 Modernization Program $3 Billion Over Budget |last=Magnuson |first=Stew |date=31 July 2026 |work=National Defense |access-date=4 August 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-26-109272.pdf |title=B-52 BOMBER Air Force Actions Needed to Improve Major Modernization and Sustainment Efforts |date=July 2026 |work=GAO Report to Congressional Committees |access-date=4 August 2026}}</ref>

The B-52 is due to receive a range of upgrades alongside a planned engine retrofit. These upgrades aim to modernize the sensors and displays of the B-52. They include the new variant of the [[AN/APG-79]] active [[Active electronically scanned array|electronically scanned array]] radar designated the [[AN/APQ-188]], replacing older mechanically scanned arrays, the streamlining of the nose, and the deletion of blisters housing the forward-looking infrared/electro-optical viewing system. In October 2022, Boeing released new images of what the upgrade would look like.<ref>{{cite web |title=New Image Shows How B-52 Will Look After Engine, Radar Replacement |website=Air and Space Forces Magazine |url=https://www.airandspaceforces.com/new-image-shows-how-b-52h-will-look-after-engine-radar-replacement/ |date=19 October 2022}}</ref><ref name=Air-Space_new_designation/> The upgrades will also include improved communication systems, new pylons, new cockpit displays, and the deletion of one crew station. The changes will carry the designation B-52J.<ref name=Air-Space_new_designation>{{cite web |title=B-52 Will Get at Least One New Designation With Radar, Engine Upgrades |url=https://www.airandspaceforces.com/b-52-will-get-at-least-one-new-designation-with-radar-engine-upgrades/ |date=10 August 2022 |work=Air and Space Forces Magazine}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://breakingdefense.com/2022/08/with-new-radar-and-engines-in-sight-the-b-52-gets-ready-for-largest-modification-in-its-history/ |title=With new radar and engines in sight, the B-52 gets ready for 'largest modification in its history' |date=26 August 2022}}</ref> The B-52J is scheduled to reach Initial operational capability in 2033.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.airandspaceforces.com/gao-b-52j-initial-operational-capability-three-year-delay/|title=New Report: B-52J Initial Operational Capability Slips to 2033|first=John|last=Tirpak|date=18 June 2024|website=Air & Space Forces Magazine}}</ref>

==Design==
[[File:B-17, 29 &amp; 52, Barksdale 2017 02.jpg|thumb|[[B-29 Superfortress]] ''[[Doc (aircraft)|Doc]]'', [[B-17 Flying Fortress]] ''[[Thunderbird (aircraft)|Thunderbird]]'', and a B-52 Stratofortress flying in formation at the 2017 [[Barksdale Air Force Base]] Airshow]]

===Overview===
The B-52 shared many technological similarities with the preceding B-47 Stratojet strategic bomber. The two aircraft used the same basic design, such as swept wings and podded jet engines,{{sfn|Tegler|2000|p=xiii}} and the cabin included the crew ejection systems.{{sfn|Tegler|2000|pp=84–85}} On the B-52D, the pilots and electronic countermeasures (ECM) operator ejected upwards, while the lower deck crew ejected downwards; until the B-52G, the gunner had to jettison the tail gun to [[parachute|bail out]].{{sfn|Higham|2005|pp=43–44}} The tail gunner in early model B-52s was located in the traditional location in the tail of the plane, with both visual and [[Fire-control radar|radar gun laying]] systems; in later models, the gunner was moved to the front of the fuselage, with gun laying carried out by radar alone, much like the B-58 Hustler's tail gun system.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thedrive.com/the-war-zone/10237/the-u-s-air-forces-last-tail-gunner-has-retired |title=The U.S. Air Force's Last Tail Gunner Has Retired |last=Trevithick |first=Joseph |website=The Drive |date=12 May 2017 |access-date=1 July 2019}}</ref>

[[Fatigue (material)|Structural fatigue]] was accelerated by at least a factor of eight in a low-altitude flight profile over that of high-altitude flying, requiring costly repairs to extend service life. In the early 1960s, the three-phase ''High Stress'' program was launched to counter structural fatigue, enrolling aircraft at 2,000 flying hours.{{sfnm|1a1=Tagg|1y=2004|1p=87|2a1=Knaack|2y=1988|2pp=254–255}} Follow-up programs were conducted, such as a 2,000-hour service life extension to select airframes in 1966–1968, and the extensive ''Pacer Plank'' reskinning, completed in 1977.{{sfnm|1a1=Lake|1y=2003b|1p=101|2a1=Knaack|2y=1988|2p=259}} The [[wet wing]] introduced on G and H models was even more susceptible to fatigue, experiencing 60% more stress during a flight than the old wing. The wings were modified by 1964 under ''ECP 1050''.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=276–277}} This was followed by a fuselage skin and [[longeron]] replacement (''ECP 1185'') in 1966, and the ''B-52 Stability Augmentation and Flight Control'' program (''ECP 1195'') in 1967.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=276–277}} Fuel leaks due to deteriorating [[Marman clamp]]s continued to plague all variants of the B-52. To this end, all aircraft variants were subjected to ''Blue Band'' (1957), ''Hard Shell'' (1958), and finally ''QuickClip'' (1958) programs. The latter fitted safety straps that prevented catastrophic loss of fuel in case of clamp failure.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=266–267}} The B-52's service ceiling is officially listed as {{convert|50000|ft|m}}, but operational experience shows this is difficult to reach when fully laden with bombs. According to one source: "The optimal altitude for a combat mission was around {{convert|43000|ft|m}}, because to exceed that height would rapidly degrade the plane's range."{{sfn|Sorenson|1995|p=127}}

[[File:Boeing B-52 with no vertical stabilizer.jpg|thumb|left|B-52H (AF Ser. No. 61-23), configured at the time as a [[testbed]] to investigate structural failures, still flying after its [[vertical stabilizer]] sheared off in severe turbulence on 10 January 1964. The aircraft landed safely.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Tinker |first=Frank A |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=69cDAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA94 |title=Who Will Bell the Invisible CAT? |magazine=[[Popular Mechanics]] |date=August 1969 |pages=94–97}}</ref> |alt=Black-and-white photo of a B-52 in flight with its vertical stabilizer sheared off.]]
In September 2006, the B-52 became one of the first US military aircraft to fly using alternative fuel. It took off from [[Edwards Air Force Base]] with a 50/50 blend of [[Fischer–Tropsch process]] (FT) synthetic fuel and conventional [[JP-8]] jet fuel, which burned in two of the eight engines. On 15 December 2006, a B-52 took off from Edwards with the synthetic fuel powering all eight engines, the first time a USAF aircraft was entirely powered by the blend. The seven-hour flight was considered a success. This program is part of the Department of Defense [[Assured Fuel Initiative]], which aimed to reduce crude oil usage and obtain half of its aviation fuel from alternative sources by 2016.<ref>{{Cite web |title=B-52 flight uses synthetic fuel in all eight engines |url=https://www.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/128702/b-52-flight-uses-synthetic-fuel-in-all-eight-engines |date=16 December 2006 |access-date=1 March 2023 |publisher=United States Air Force}}</ref> On 8 August 2007, Air Force Secretary [[Michael Wynne]] certified the B-52H as fully approved to use the FT blend.<ref>{{Cite web |title=SECAF certifies synthetic fuel blends for B-52H |url=https://www.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/126057/secaf-certifies-synthetic-fuel-blends-for-b-52h/ |date=7 August 2007 |access-date=1 March 2023 |publisher=United States Air Force}}</ref>

=== Flight controls ===
Because of the B-52's mission parameters, only modest maneuvers would be required with no need for [[Spin (aerodynamics)|spin]] recovery. The aircraft has a relatively small, narrow [[Chord (aeronautics)|chord]] rudder, giving it limited [[Aircraft principal axes|yaw]] control authority. Originally an all-moving [[vertical stabilizer]] was to be used but was abandoned because of doubts about hydraulic actuator reliability.{{sfn|Abzug|Larrabee|2002|pages=105–108}} Because the aircraft has eight engines, [[Critical engine|asymmetrical thrust]] due to the loss of an engine in flight would be minimal and correctable with the narrow rudder. To assist with crosswind takeoffs and landings the main [[landing gear]] can be pivoted 20 degrees to either side from neutral.{{sfn|Jenkins|Rogers|1990|p=15}} The crew would preset the yaw adjustable crosswind landing gear according to wind observations made on the ground.

Like the rudder, the [[Elevator (aeronautics)|elevator]] is also very narrow chord and the B-52 suffers from limited elevator control authority. For long-term pitch trim and airspeed changes the aircraft uses a [[stabilator]] (or all-moving tail) with the elevator used for small adjustments within a stabilizer setting. The stabilizer is adjustable through 13 degrees of movement (nine up, four down) and is crucial to operations during takeoff and landing due to large pitch changes induced by [[Flap (aeronautics)|flap]] application.<ref name=aer>{{cite web |url=http://www.aer.ita.br/~bmattos/mundo/bomb/b52.htm |title=Boeing B-52 Stratofortress |author=Bento Silva de Mattos |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150202052322/http://www.aer.ita.br/~bmattos/mundo/bomb/b52.htm |archive-date=2 February 2015}}</ref>

B-52s prior to the G models had very small [[ailerons]] with a short span that was approximately equal to their chord. These "feeler ailerons" were used to provide feedback forces to the pilot's control yoke and to fine-tune the roll axes during delicate maneuvers such as aerial refueling. Due to twisting of the thin main wing, conventional outboard flap-type ailerons would lose authority and therefore could not be used. In other words, aileron activation would cause the wing to twist, undermining roll control. Six [[spoileron]]s on each wing are responsible for the majority of roll control. The late B-52G models eliminated the ailerons altogether and added an extra spoileron to each wing.{{sfn|Abzug|Larrabee|2002|pages=105–108}} Partly because of the lack of ailerons, the B-52G and H models were more susceptible to [[Dutch roll]].<ref name=aer/>

===Avionics===
[[File:B-52 lower deck.jpg|thumb|A view of the lower deck of the B-52, dubbed the battle station|alt=Lower deck of a B-52, with instruments and displays featuring dominantly on the aircraft's side wall. Two crew members man this station.]]

Ongoing problems with avionics systems were addressed in the ''Jolly Well'' program, completed in 1964, which improved components of the AN/ASQ-38 bombing navigational computer and the terrain computer. The MADREC (Malfunction Detection and Recording) upgrade fitted to most aircraft by 1965 could detect failures in avionics and weapons computer systems and was essential in monitoring the [[AGM-28 Hound Dog]] missiles. The [[electronic countermeasure]]s capability of the B-52 was expanded with ''Rivet Rambler'' (1971) and ''Rivet Ace'' (1973).{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=279–280}}

To improve operations at low altitudes, the AN/ASQ-151 Electro-Optical Viewing System (EVS), which consisted of an AN/AVQ-22 [[low light level television]] (LLLTV) and an AN/AAQ-6 [[forward looking infrared]] (FLIR){{r|RivetAce}} system mounted in blisters under the noses of B-52Gs and Hs between 1972 and 1976.{{sfn|Willis|2005b|pp=41–43}} The navigational capabilities of the B-52 were later augmented with the addition of [[GPS]] in the 1980s.{{sfn|Condor|1994|p=38}} The [[IBM AP-101]], also used on the [[Rockwell B-1 Lancer]] bomber and the [[Space Shuttle]], was the B-52's main computer.<ref name="ap101" />

In 2007, the [[LITENING targeting pod]] was fitted, which increased the effectiveness of the aircraft in the attack of ground targets with a variety of standoff weapons, using [[laser guidance]], a high-resolution forward-looking infrared sensor ([[FLIR]]), and a [[Charge-coupled device|CCD camera]] used to obtain target imagery.<ref>{{cite report |last=Hopper |first=David. |url=http://wstiac.alionscience.com/pdf/eNews_WSMR_041808.pdf |title=Upgraded B-52 Still on Cutting Edge |publisher=Weapon System Technology Information Analysis Center (WSTIAC) |date=31 March 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110707111918/http://wstiac.alionscience.com/pdf/eNews_WSMR_041808.pdf |archive-date=7 July 2011}}</ref> LITENING pods have been fitted to a wide variety of other US aircraft, such as the [[McDonnell Douglas F/A-18 Hornet]], the [[General Dynamics F-16 Fighting Falcon]] and the [[McDonnell Douglas AV-8B Harrier II]].<ref>{{cite journal |last=Neuenswander |first=David |title=Joint Laser Interoperability, Tomorrow's Answer to Precision Engagement |journal=Air & Space Power Journal |date=28 June 2001 |url=https://www.airuniversity.af.edu/Portals/10/ASPJ/journals/Chronicles/neuenswander.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110213195627/http://www.airpower.maxwell.af.mil/airchronicles/cc/neuenswander.html |archive-date=13 February 2011 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Armament===
[[File:Boeing B-52D-40-BW (SN 56-0695) and GAM-72 Quail decoy missile and trailer 061127-F-1234S-010.jpg|thumb|left|A B-52D with [[anti-flash white]] on the underside]]

The ability to carry up to 20 [[AGM-69 SRAM]] nuclear missiles was added to G and H models, starting in 1971.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=277–278}} To further improve its offensive ability, air-launched cruise missiles (ALCMs) were fitted.{{sfn|Tagg|2004|p=89}} After testing of both the USAF-backed [[AGM-86 ALCM|Boeing AGM-86 Air Launched Cruise Missile]] and the Navy-backed [[BGM-109 Tomahawk|General Dynamics AGM-109 Tomahawk]], the AGM-86B was selected for operation by the B-52 (and ultimately by the B-1 Lancer).{{sfn|Polmar|2005|p=529}} A total of 194 B-52Gs and Hs were modified to carry AGM-86s, carrying 12 missiles on underwing pylons, with 82 B-52Hs further modified to carry another eight missiles on a rotary launcher fitted in the bomb bay. To conform with [[Strategic Arms Limitation Talks|SALT II Treaty]] requirements that cruise missile-capable aircraft be readily identifiable by reconnaissance satellites, the cruise missile-armed B-52Gs were modified with a distinctive [[wing root]] fairing. As all B-52Hs were assumed modified, no visual modification of these aircraft was required.{{sfn|Willis|2005b|pp=44–45}} In 1990, the stealthy [[AGM-129 ACM]] cruise missile entered service; although intended to replace the AGM-86, the high cost and the Cold War's end led to only 450 being produced; unlike the AGM-86, no conventional, non-nuclear version was built.{{sfn|Dorr|Rogers|1996|pp=65–66}} The B-52 was to have been modified to utilize [[Northrop Grumman]]'s [[AGM-137 TSSAM]] weapon; however, the missile was canceled due to development costs.{{sfn|Polmar|2005|p=532}}

[[File:B-52H Bomb Bay - 1984 at Ellsworth AFB.jpg|thumb|B-52H bomb bay: [[AGM-69 SRAM]] missiles (front, white) and [[B28 nuclear bomb]]s (background, gray), as a downloading takes place during Exercise GLOBAL SHIELD '84]]
Those B-52Gs not converted as cruise missile carriers underwent a series of modifications to improve conventional bombing. They were fitted with a new Integrated Conventional Stores Management System (ICSMS) and new underwing pylons that could hold larger bombs or other stores than the external pylons could. Thirty B-52Gs were further modified to carry up to 12 [[AGM-84 Harpoon|AGM-84 Harpoon anti-ship missile]]s each, while 12 B-52Gs were fitted to carry the [[AGM-142 Have Nap]] stand-off air-to-ground missile.{{sfn|Lake||2003b|pp=108–109}} When the B-52G was retired in 1994, an urgent scheme was launched to restore an interim Harpoon and Have Nap capability,{{refn|The Have Nap missile, carried only by the B-52, enabled stand-off attacks on targets while maintaining a "man-in-the-loop" guidance system capability.{{sfnm|1a1=Dorr|1a2=Rogers|1y=1996|1pp=78–79|2a1=Lake|2y=2001|2p=290}}|group=Note}} the four aircraft being modified to carry Harpoon and four to carry Have Nap under the ''Rapid Eight'' program.{{sfn|Lake|2003b|p=114}}

[[File:A striking challenge (7851986672).jpg|thumb|left|Members of the Global Strike Challenge nuclear/conventional load crew team lift a GBU-31 Joint Direct Attack Munition into position during a dry run leading up to the 2012 Air Force Global Strike Command Challenge on Barksdale Air Force Base, Louisiana, 20 Aug 2012]]
The Conventional Enhancement Modification (CEM) program gave the B-52H a more comprehensive conventional weapons capability, adding the modified underwing weapon pylons used by conventional-armed B-52Gs, Harpoon and Have Nap, and the capability to carry new-generation weapons including the [[Joint Direct Attack Munition]] (JDAM) and [[Wind Corrected Munitions Dispenser]] guided bombs, the [[AGM-154 Joint Standoff Weapon|AGM-154]] glide bomb and the [[AGM-158 JASSM]] missile. The CEM program also introduced new radios, integrated [[Global Positioning System]] into the aircraft's navigation system, and replaced the under-nose FLIR with a more modern unit. Forty-seven B-52Hs were modified under the CEM program by 1996, with 19 more by the end of 1999.{{sfnm|1a1=Lake|1y=2001|1pp=290–291|2a1=Dorr|2a2=Rogers|2y=1996|2pp=81–82}}

By around 2010, [[US Strategic Command]] stopped assigning [[B61 nuclear bomb|B61]] and [[B83 nuclear bomb|B83]] nuclear gravity bombs to B-52, and later listed only the [[Northrop Grumman B-2 Spirit|B-2]] as tasked with delivering strategic nuclear bombs in budget requests. Nuclear gravity bombs were removed from the B-52's capabilities because it is no longer considered survivable enough to penetrate modern air defenses, instead relying on nuclear cruise missiles and focusing on expanding its conventional strike role.<ref>{{Cite web |first=Hans |last=Kristensen |date=25 May 2017 |title=B-52 Bomber No Longer Delivers Nuclear Gravity Bombs |url=https://fas.org/blogs/security/2017/05/b-52-bombs/ |access-date=1 March 2023 |website=Federation of American Scientists}}</ref> The 2019 "Safety Rules for U.S. Strategic Bomber Aircraft" manual subsequently confirmed the removal of B61-7 and B83-1 gravity bombs from the B-52H's approved weapons configuration.<ref name="B61/B83 removal">{{cite web |first=Joseph |last=Trevithick |date=19 January 2020 |url=https://www.thedrive.com/the-war-zone/31825/the-air-forces-b-52h-bomber-force-has-said-goodbye-to-its-nuclear-bombs |title=The Air Force's B-52H Bomber Force Has Said Goodbye To Its Nuclear Bombs |website=The War Zone |publisher=TheDrive.com |access-date=14 January 2020}}</ref>

Starting in 2016, Boeing is to upgrade the internal rotary launchers to the [[MIL-STD-1760]] interface to enable the internal carriage of smart bombs, which previously could be carried only on the wings.<ref>{{cite news |last=Raatz |first=Joseph |publisher=United States Air Force |title=Upgrade gives B-52 more teeth |url=https://www.afgsc.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/629758/upgrade-gives-b-52-more-teeth/ |date=20 September 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130927054852/http://www.afgsc.af.mil/news/story.asp?id=123364101 |archive-date=27 September 2013}}</ref>{{update inline|date=November 2024}}

While the B-1 Lancer has a larger theoretical maximum payload of {{convert|75000|lb}} compared to the B-52's {{convert|70000|lb}}, the bombers are rarely able to carry their full loads. The most the B-52 carries is a full load of AGM-86Bs totaling {{convert|62660|lb}}. The B-1 has the internal weapons bay space to carry more GBU-31 JDAMs and JASSMs, but the B-52 upgraded with the conventional rotary launcher can carry more of other JDAM variants.<ref>{{cite news |first=Oriana |last=Pawlyk |url=https://www.military.com/daily-news/2018/02/12/make-way-b-21-air-force-plans-retire-b-1-b-2-2030s.html |title=To Make Way for Future Bomber, AF Plans to Retire B-1, B-2 in 2030s |work=Military.com |date=12 February 2018}}</ref>

The [[AGM-183A]] Air-Launched Rapid Response (ARRW) [[hypersonic]] missile and the future [[Long Range Stand Off Weapon|Long Range Stand Off]] (LRSO) [[Nuclear weapon|nuclear]]-armed [[air-launched cruise missile]] will join the B-52 inventory in the future.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.defensenews.com/smr/global-strike/2019/09/15/here-are-some-of-the-upgrades-coming-to-the-us-air-forces-oldest-bomber/ |first=Valerie |last=Insinna |title=Here are some of the upgrades coming to the US Air Force's oldest bomber |date=15 September 2020}}</ref>

===Engines===
[[File:USAF B-52 Stratofortress Engines.jpg|thumb|USAF B-52H Stratofortress TF33 turbofan engines]]

The eight engines of the B-52 are paired in pods and suspended by four pylons beneath and forward of the wings' [[leading edge]]. The careful arrangement of the pylons also allowed them to work as [[wing fence]]s and delay the onset of [[Stall (fluid dynamics)|stall]]. The first two prototypes, XB-52 and YB-52, were both powered by experimental [[Pratt & Whitney J57|Pratt & Whitney YJ57-P-3]] turbojet engines with {{convert|8700|lbf|kN}} of static thrust each.<ref name=aer />

The B-52A models were equipped with Pratt & Whitney J57-P-1W turbojets, providing a dry thrust of {{convert|10000|lbf|kN}} which could be increased for short periods to {{convert|11000|lbf|kN}} with [[Water injection (engine)|water injection]]. The water was carried in a {{convert|360|usgal|impgal L|adj=on}} tank in the rear fuselage.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Boeing B-52 Stratofortress Airplane Videos and Airplane Pictures |url=http://www.livingwarbirds.com/boeing-b52.php |access-date=13 December 2022 |website=Living Warbirds}} {{Dead link|date=September 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

B-52B, C, D and E models were equipped with Pratt & Whitney J57-P-29W, J57-P-29WA, or J57-P-19W series engines, all rated at {{cvt|10500|lbf|kN}}. The B-52F and G models were powered by Pratt & Whitney J57-P-43WB turbojets, each rated at {{convert|13750|lbf|kN}} static thrust with water injection.

On 9 May 1961, the B-52H began to be delivered to the USAF with cleaner-burning and quieter [[Pratt & Whitney JT3D|Pratt & Whitney TF33-P-3]] turbofans with a maximum thrust of {{convert|17100|lbf|kN}}.<ref name=aer />

====Engine retrofit====
In a study for the USAF in the mid-1970s, Boeing investigated replacing the engines, changing to a new wing, and other improvements to upgrade B-52G/H aircraft as an alternative to the B-1A, then in development.{{sfn|Jenkins|1999|p=39}}

In 1996, Rolls-Royce and Boeing jointly proposed fitting each B-52 with four leased [[Rolls-Royce RB211]] engines. This would have involved replacing the eight Pratt & Whitney TF33 engines (total thrust {{cvt|136000|lbf|kN}}) with four RB211-535E4 engines (total thrust {{cvt|172,400|lbf|kN}}), which would increase range and reduce fuel consumption.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rolfsen |first=Bruce |date=15 April 2002 |title=The B-52's big 5-0; The Stratofortress cuts a wide swath – and may see another 40 years' service |work=Air Force Times |quote=The new engines are more fuel efficient and would increase the jet's total engine thrust to 172,400 pounds, nearly a two- thirds improvement over TF33 engines, advocates said. Air Force officials are unconvinced that savings in fuel costs or tanker sorties would justify the new engines. In its 1996 proposal, Boeing estimated the Air Force would save {{US$|4.7}}{{nbsp}}billion by leasing the new engines for 71 B-52s}}</ref>{{sfn|Lake|2003b|p=115}} However, a USAF analysis in 1997 concluded that Boeing's estimated savings of {{US$|4.7}}{{nbsp}}billion would not be realized and that reengining would instead cost {{US$|1.3}}{{nbsp}}billion more than keeping the existing engines, citing significant up-front procurement and re-tooling expenditure.<ref name="NSIAD-97-210BR"/>

The USAF's 1997 rejection of reengining was subsequently disputed in a [[Defense Science Board]] (DSB) report in 2003. The DSB urged the USAF to re-engine the aircraft without delay,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://aviationweek.com/awin/aair-force-widens-review-b-52-re-engining-options |title=Air Force widens review of B-52 reengining options |work=[[Aviation Week]] |date=7 July 2003}} {{Subscription required}}</ref> saying doing so would not only create significant cost savings but reduce [[Environmental impact of aviation|greenhouse gas emissions]] and increase aircraft range and endurance; these conclusions were in line with the conclusions of a separate Congress-funded study conducted in 2003. Criticizing the USAF cost analysis, the DSB found that among other things, the USAF failed to account for the cost of aerial refueling; the DSB estimated that aerial refueling cost {{convert|17.50|$/USgal|$/l}}, whereas the USAF had failed to account for the cost of delivering the fuel and so had only priced fuel at {{convert|1.20|$/USgal}}.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.acq.osd.mil/dsb/reports/2000s/ADA428790.pdf |title=Defense Science Board Task Force on B-52H Re-Engining |publisher=[[Defense Science Board]] |date=June 2004 |access-date=2 July 2017 |archive-date=29 April 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170429175925/http://www.acq.osd.mil/dsb/reports/2000s/ADA428790.pdf}}</ref>

On 23 April 2020, the USAF released its request for proposals for 608 commercial engines plus spares and support equipment, with the plan to award the contract in May 2021.<ref name= fgRFP>{{cite news |url=https://www.flightglobal.com/fixed-wing/us-air-force-issues-draft-request-for-proposal-to-replace-b-52-engines/138099.article |title=US Air Force issues draft request for proposal to replace B-52 engines |work=Flight International |first=Garrett |last=Reim |date=27 April 2020 |access-date=28 April 2020}}</ref> This Commercial Engine Reengining Program (CERP) saw General Electric propose its [[General Electric CF34|CF34-10]] and [[General Electric Passport|Passport]] turbofans, Pratt & Whitney its [[Pratt & Whitney Canada PW800|PW800]], and Rolls-Royce its BR725 to be designated [[Rolls-Royce BR700#BR725|F130]].<ref name= fgRFP/> On 24 September 2021, the USAF selected the Rolls-Royce F130 as the winner and announced plans to purchase 650 engines (608 direct replacements and 42 spares), for {{US$|2.6}}{{nbsp}}billion.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.airforcemag.com/rolls-royce-wins-b-52-re-engining-program-worth-2-6-billion/ |title=Rolls-Royce Wins B-52 Reengining Program Worth $2.6 Billion |last=Tirpak |first=John |date=24 September 2021 |work=Air Force Magazine |access-date=26 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210925230231/https://www.airforcemag.com/rolls-royce-wins-b-52-re-engining-program-worth-2-6-billion/ |archive-date=25 September 2021 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="Tirpak 2023"/>

Unlike the previous re-engine proposal, which also involved reducing the number of engines from eight to four, the F130 re-engine program maintains eight engines on the B-52. Although four-engine operation would be more efficient, retrofitting the airframe to operate with only four engines would involve additional changes to the aircraft's systems and control surfaces (particularly the rudder), thereby increasing the time, cost, and complexity of the project.<ref name="Tirpak 2019">{{cite news |last=Tirpak |first=John |title=Reengining the B-52 |url=https://www.airforcemag.com/article/re-engining-the-b-52 |work=Air Force Magazine |date=21 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211219192649/https://www.airforcemag.com/article/re-engining-the-b-52/ |archive-date=19 December 2021 |access-date=19 December 2021 |url-status=dead}}</ref> B-52Hs upgraded with Rolls Royce F130 engines will be redesignated as "B-52Js".<ref name="Tirpak 2023"/>

