==Operational history==
===Introduction===
Although the B-52A was the first production variant, these aircraft were used only in testing. The first operational version was the B-52B, which had been developed in parallel with the prototypes since 1951. First flying in December 1954, B-52B, AF Serial Number 52-8711, entered operational service with [[93rd Heavy Bombardment Wing]] (93rd BW) at [[Castle Air Force Base]], California, on 29 June 1955. The wing became operational on 12 March 1956. The training for B-52 crews consisted of five weeks of ground school and four weeks of flying, accumulating 35 to 50 hours in the air. The new B-52Bs replaced operational B-36s on a one-to-one basis.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=234–237}}

Early operations were problematic;{{sfn|Boyne|2001|p=216}} in addition to supply problems, there were also technical issues.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=237}} Ramps and taxiways deteriorated under the aircraft's weight, the fuel system was prone to leaks and icing,{{sfn|Boyne|2001|p=220}} and bombing and fire control computers were unreliable.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=237}} The split-level cockpit presented a temperature control problem{{nbsp}}– the pilots' cockpit was heated by sunlight while the observer and the navigator on the bottom deck sat on the ice-cold floor. Thus, a comfortable temperature setting for the pilots caused the other crew members to freeze, while a comfortable temperature for the bottom crew caused the pilots to overheat.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=238}} The J57 engines proved unreliable. [[Alternator]] failure caused the first fatal B-52 crash in February 1956;{{sfn|Lake|2003a|p=119}} as a result, the fleet was briefly grounded. In July, fuel and hydraulic issues grounded the B-52s again. In response to maintenance issues, the USAF set up "Sky Speed" teams of 50 contractors at each B-52 base to perform maintenance and routine checkups, taking an average of one week per aircraft.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=240}}

[[File:B-52-castleafb-1957.jpg|thumb|alt=Black-and-white photo of three B-52s parked close together facing left, as personnel on the ground prepare them for departure|Three B-52Bs of the 93rd Bomb Wing prepare to depart [[March Air Force Base]] for Castle Air Force Base, California, after their record-setting round-the-world flight in 1957]]
On 21 May 1956, a B-52B (52–13) dropped a [[Mark 15 nuclear bomb|Mk-15 nuclear bomb]] over the [[Bikini Atoll]] in a test code-named [[Operation Redwing|Cherokee]]. It was the first air-dropped [[thermonuclear weapon]].{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=243}} This aircraft now is on display at the [[National Museum of Nuclear Science and History]] in [[Albuquerque]], [[New Mexico]]. From 24 to 25 November 1956, four B-52Bs of the 93rd BW and four B-52Cs of the 42nd BW flew nonstop around the perimeter of North America in ''[[Operation Quick Kick (1956)|Operation Quick Kick]]'', which covered {{convert|15530|mi|nmi km}} in 31 hours, 30 minutes. SAC noted the flight time could have been reduced by 5 to 6 hours had the four inflight refuelings been done by fast jet-powered tanker aircraft rather than propeller-driven [[Boeing KC-97 Stratofreighter]]s.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=244}} In a demonstration of the B-52's global reach, from 16 to 18 January 1957, three B-52Bs made a [[non-stop flight]] around the world during ''[[Operation Power Flite]]'', during which {{convert|24325|mi|nmi km}} was covered in 45 hours 19 minutes ({{cvt|536.8|mph|km/h|disp=or}}) with several in-flight refuelings by KC-97s.{{sfn|Condor|1994|p=42}}{{refn|The 93rd Bomb Wing received the [[Mackay Trophy]] for accomplishing their round-the-world non-stop flight in January 1957.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=243}}|group=Note}}

The B-52 set many records over the next few years. On 26 September 1958, a B-52D set a world speed record of {{convert|560.705|mph|kn km/h}} over a {{convert|10000|km|mi nmi|abbr=off|sp=us}} closed circuit without a payload. The same day, another B-52D established a world speed record of {{convert|597.675|mph|kn km/h}} over a {{convert|5000|km|mi nmi|abbr=off|sp=us}} closed circuit without a payload.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=259}} On 14 December 1960, a B-52G set a world distance record by flying unrefueled for {{convert|10078.84|mi|nmi km}}; the flight lasted 19 hours 44 minutes ({{cvt|510.75|mph|km/h|disp=or}}).{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=282}} From 10 to 11 January 1962, a B-52H (60–40) set a world distance record by flying unrefueled, surpassing the prior B-52 record set two years earlier, from [[Kadena Air Base]], [[Okinawa Prefecture]], Japan, to [[Torrejón Air Base]], Spain, which covered {{convert|12532.28|mi|nmi km}}.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=289}}<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thisdayinaviation.com/11-january-1962/ |title=11 January 1962 |website=This Day in Aviation |date=11 January 2017}}</ref> The flight passed over Seattle, Fort Worth and the Azores.

===Cold War===
{{Main|Cold War}}
[[File:Operation Chrome Dome route.jpg|thumb|Southerly route of the [[Operation Chrome Dome]] airborne nuclear alert|alt=Diagram of the route that nuclear bomb-carrying B-52s would take to enemy countries. It follows the Mediterranean Sea, and passes over Italy before turning north over the Adriatic Sea.]]

When the B-52 entered service, the [[Strategic Air Command]] (SAC) intended to use it to deter and counteract the vast and modernizing [[Soviet Union]]'s military. As the Soviet Union increased its nuclear capabilities, destroying or "countering" the forces that would deliver nuclear strikes (bombers, missiles, etc.) became of great strategic importance.{{sfn|Tillman|2007|p=100}} The [[Eisenhower administration]] endorsed this switch in focus; the President in 1954 expressed a preference for military targets over civilian ones, a principle reinforced in the [[Single Integrated Operation Plan]] (SIOP), a plan of action in the case of nuclear war breaking out.<ref>{{cite journal |jstor=2626731 |title=The Origins of Overkill: Nuclear Weapons and American Strategy, 1945–1960 |journal=[[International Security (journal)|International Security]] |volume=7 |issue=4 |pages=3–71 |last1=Rosenberg |first1=David A |date=Spring 1983 |doi=10.2307/2626731 |s2cid=154529784}}</ref>

Throughout the [[Cold War]], B-52s and other US strategic bombers performed airborne alert patrols under code names such as [[Operation Head Start|Head Start]], [[Operation Chrome Dome|Chrome Dome]], [[Thule Monitor Mission|Hard Head]], [[Operation Round Robin|Round Robin]] and [[Operation Giant Lance|Giant Lance]]. Bombers [[Loiter (flight)|loitered]] at high altitudes near the borders of the Soviet Union to provide rapid first strike or retaliation capability in case of nuclear war.<ref>{{cite web |last=Kristensen |first=Hans M |url=http://www.nukestrat.com/dk/alert.htm |title=The Airborne Alert Program Over Greenland |website=The Nuclear Information Project |access-date=2 October 2011 |archive-date=24 January 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120124132255/http://www.nukestrat.com/dk/alert.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref> These airborne patrols formed one component of the US's nuclear deterrent, which would act to prevent the breakout of a large-scale war between the US and the Soviet Union under the concept of [[Mutually Assured Destruction]].<ref>{{cite journal |last=Parrington |first=Alan J |title=Mutually Assured Destruction Revisited, Strategic Doctrine in Question |journal=[[Airpower Journal]] |date=Winter 1997 |url=https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/pdfs/ADA529841.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220307181533/https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/pdfs/ADA529841.pdf |archive-date=7 March 2022}}</ref>

Due to the late 1950s-era threat of [[surface-to-air missile]]s (SAMs) that could threaten high-altitude aircraft,{{sfnm|1a1=Jenkins|1y=1999|1p=21|2a1=Spick|2y=1986|2pp=4–5}} seen in practice in the [[1960 U-2 incident]],<ref>{{cite news |title=May 1960 – The U-2 Incident. – Soviet and American Statements |url=http://www.keesings.com/search?kssp_selected_tab=article&kssp_a_id=17425n01sov |work=[[Keesing's Record of World Events]] |volume=6 |year=1960}}</ref> the intended use of B-52 was changed to serve as a [[Nap-of-the-earth|low-level]] penetration bomber during a foreseen attack upon the Soviet Union, as [[terrain mask#Terrain masking|terrain masking]] provided an effective method of avoiding radar and thus the threat of the SAMs.{{sfn|Spick|1986|pp=6–8}} The aircraft was planned to fly towards the target at {{cvt|400-440|mph|abbr=on}} and deliver their weapons from {{cvt|400|ft}} or lower.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Jones |first=Tom |title=A Full Retaliatory Response |magazine=[[Smithsonian (magazine)|Smithsonian]] |date=November 2005 |url=https://www.airspacemag.com/history-of-flight/a-full-retaliatory-response-6909238/}}</ref> Although never intended for the low-level role, the B-52's flexibility allowed it to outlast several intended successors as the nature of aerial warfare changed. The B-52's large airframe enabled the addition of multiple design improvements, new equipment, and other adaptations over its service life.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=279-180}}

[[File:Nuclear Might Ready but Safe (1965).webm|thumb|1965 United States military nuclear weapons safety handling and aerial delivery training film.]]
In November 1959, to improve the aircraft's combat capabilities in the changing strategic environment, SAC initiated the ''Big Four'' modification program (also known as ''Modification 1000'') for all operational B-52s except early B models.{{sfnm|1a1=Tagg|1y=2004|1p=87|2a1=Spick|2y=1986|2pp=6–8}} The program was completed by 1963.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=252–254}} The four modifications were the ability to launch AGM-28 Hound Dog standoff nuclear missiles and [[ADM-20 Quail]] decoys, an advanced electronic countermeasures (ECM) suite, and upgrades to perform the all-weather, low-altitude (below 500&nbsp;feet or 150&nbsp;m) interdiction mission in the face of advancing Soviet missile-based air defenses.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=252–254}}

In the 1960s, there were concerns over the fleet's capable lifespan. Several projects beyond the B-52, the [[Convair B-58 Hustler]] and [[North American XB-70 Valkyrie]], had either been aborted or proved disappointing in light of changing requirements, which left the older B-52 as the main bomber as opposed to the planned successive aircraft models.{{sfnm|1a1=Miller|1y=1985|1pp=69–70|2a1=Greenwood|2y=1995|2p=289}} On 19 February 1965, General Curtis E. LeMay testified to Congress that the lack of a follow-up bomber project to the B-52 raised the danger that, "The B-52 is going to fall apart on us before we can get a replacement for it."<ref>{{cite book |title=Astronautics and Aeronautics, 1965: Chronology on Science, Technology, and Policy |year=1963 |publisher=National Aeronautics and Space Administration |via=[[NASA]] Historical Staff, Office of Policy Analysis |id=NASA SP-4006 |page=84 |access-date=5 June 2010 |url=https://archive.org/details/astronauticsaero65unit/page/83}}</ref> Other aircraft, such as the [[General Dynamics F-111 Aardvark]], later complemented the B-52 in roles the aircraft was not as capable in, such as missions involving high-speed, low-level penetration dashes.{{sfn|Schwartz|1998|p=119}}

===Vietnam War===
{{See also|Vietnam War}}
[[File:СВС у обломков сбитого Б-52 в окрестностях Ханоя 23.12.1972 (1).jpg|thumb|Soviet specialists inspect the wreckage of the B-52 Stratofortress shot down near Hanoi on 23 December 1972.]]

With the escalating situation in Southeast Asia, 28 B-52Fs were fitted with external racks for 24 of the {{convert|750|lb|kg}} bombs under project ''South Bay'' in June 1964; an additional 46 aircraft received similar modifications under project ''Sun Bath''.{{sfn|Lake|2003b|p=103}} In March 1965, the United States commenced [[Operation Rolling Thunder]]. The first combat mission, [[Operation Arc Light]], was flown by B-52Fs on 18 June 1965, when 30 bombers of the 9th and 441st Bombardment Squadrons struck a communist stronghold near the [[Bến Cát District]] in South Vietnam. The first wave of bombers arrived too early at a designated rendezvous point, and while maneuvering to maintain station, two B-52s collided, which resulted in the loss of both bombers and eight crewmen. The remaining bombers, minus one more that turned back due to mechanical problems, continued towards the target.<ref>{{cite news |last=Anderson |first=William |title=Guam Jets Bomb S. Viet |url=http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/585846462.html?dids=585846462:585846462&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:AI&type=historic&date=Jun+18%2C+1965&author=&pub=Chicago+Tribune&desc=GUAM+JETS+BOMB+S.+VIET&pqatl=google |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |date=18 June 1965 |archive-date=25 July 2012 |access-date=28 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120725110958/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/585846462.html?dids=585846462:585846462&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:AI&type=historic&date=Jun+18%2C+1965&author=&pub=Chicago+Tribune&desc=GUAM+JETS+BOMB+S.+VIET&pqatl=google |url-status=dead }}</ref> Twenty-seven Stratofortresses bombed a {{convert|1|by|2|mi|km|adj=on|spell=in}} target box from between {{convert|19000|and|22,000|ft|m}}, with a little more than 50% of the bombs falling within the target zone.{{sfn|Hobson|2001|pp=22–23}} The force returned to [[Andersen Air Force Base]] except for one bomber with electrical problems that recovered to [[Clark Air Base]], the mission having lasted 13 hours. Post-strike assessment by teams of South Vietnamese troops with American advisors found evidence that the Viet Cong had departed from the area before the raid, and it was suspected that infiltration of the south's forces may have tipped off the north because of the [[Army of the Republic of Vietnam|South Vietnamese Army]] troops involved in the post-strike inspection.{{sfn|Schlight|1988|p=52}}

[[File:Boeing B-52 dropping bombs.jpg|thumb|left|B-52F dropping bombs on Vietnam|alt=Against a blue sky with white clouds, a B-52F releases bombs over Vietnam]]
Beginning in late 1965, a number of B-52Ds underwent ''Big Belly'' modifications to increase bomb capacity for [[carpet bombing]]s.{{sfn|Lake|2004|p=30}} While the external payload remained at 24 of {{convert|500|lb|kg}} or {{convert|750|lb|kg}} bombs, the internal capacity increased from 27 to 84 for {{cvt|500|lb|adj=on}} bombs, or from 27 to 42 for {{cvt|750|lb|adj=on}} bombs.{{sfn|Dick|Patterson|2006|p=161}} The modification created enough capacity for a total of {{convert|60000|lb|kg}} using 108 bombs. Thus modified, B-52Ds could carry {{convert|22000|lb|kg}} more than B-52Fs.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|p=256}} Designed to replace B-52Fs, modified B-52Ds entered combat in April 1966 flying from [[Andersen Air Force Base]], [[Guam]]. Each bombing mission lasted 10 to 12 hours and included an aerial refueling by [[KC-135 Stratotanker]]s.{{sfn|Donald|1997|pp=161–162}} In spring 1967, B-52s began flying from [[U-Tapao Royal Thai Navy Airfield|U-Tapao Airfield]] in Thailand so that refueling was not required.{{sfn|Dick|Patterson|2006|p=161}}

B-52s were employed during the [[Battle of Ia Drang]] in November 1965, notable as the aircraft's first use in a tactical support role.<ref>{{cite web |last=Nguyen |first=Tin |url=https://www.historynet.com/new-look-ia-drang.htm |title=A New Look at Ia Drang |date=21 August 2018 |website=HistoryNet}}</ref>

{{quote box
| quote = "The B-52s were restricted to bombing suspected Communist bases in relatively uninhabited sections because their potency approached that of a tactical nuclear weapon. A formation of six B-52s, dropping their bombs from {{cvt|30,000|ft}}, could "take out"... almost everything within a "box" approximately five-eighths mile wide by two miles long (1km × 3.2&nbsp;km). Whenever [[Operation Arc Light|Arc Light]] struck{{nbsp}}... in the vicinity of Saigon, the city woke from the tremor..."
| source = [[Neil Sheehan]], war correspondent, writing before the mass attacks on heavily populated cities including North Vietnam's capital{{sfn|Condor|1994|p=37}}
| align = right
| width = 25%
}}
On 22 November 1972, a B-52D (55–110) from U-Tapao was hit by a SAM while on a raid over [[Vinh]]. The crew was forced to abandon the damaged aircraft over Thailand. This was the first B-52 destroyed by hostile fire.<ref>{{cite news |title=Reds Down First B-52 of War |url=http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/latimes/access/660769172.html?dids=660769172:660769172&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:AI&type=historic&date=Nov+22%2C+1972&author=&pub=Los+Angeles+Times&desc=Reds+Down+First+B-52+of+War&pqatl=google |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=22 November 1972 |archive-date=25 July 2012 |access-date=28 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120725072054/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/latimes/access/660769172.html?dids=660769172:660769172&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:AI&type=historic&date=Nov+22%2C+1972&author=&pub=Los+Angeles+Times&desc=Reds+Down+First+B-52+of+War&pqatl=google |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=22 November 1972 |title=B-52 Downed by the North; First of the War, U.S. Says |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1972/11/23/archives/b52-downed-by-the-north-first-of-the-war-us-says-b52-shot-down-by.html}}</ref>

The zenith of B-52 attacks in Vietnam was [[Operation Linebacker II]] (also known as the Christmas bombings), conducted from 18 to 29 December 1972, which consisted of waves of B-52s (mostly D models, but some Gs without jamming equipment and with a smaller bomb load). Over 12 days, B-52s flew 729 [[sortie]]s and dropped 15,237&nbsp;tons of bombs on [[Hanoi]], [[Haiphong]], and other targets in North Vietnam.{{sfnm|1a1=Dick|1a2=Patterson|1y=2006|1p=187|2a1=Condor|2y=1994|2p=38|3a1=Budiansky|3y=2004|3p=394}} Originally 42 B-52s were committed to the war; however, numbers were frequently twice this figure.{{sfn|Lake|2004|p=32}} During Operation Linebacker II, fifteen B-52s were shot down, five were heavily damaged (one crashed in Laos), and five suffered medium damage. A total of 25 crewmen were killed in these losses.{{sfn|Dorr|Peacock|2000}} During the war, 31 B-52s were lost, including ten shot down over North Vietnam.{{sfn|McCarthy|2009|p=172}}

====Air-to-air combat====
[[File:Houston County, GA, USA - panoramio (100).jpg|thumb|Tail armament of a B-52D, the model used on both occasions that a B-52 scored an aerial victory. In later models, the tail gunner was moved from the conventional position to the forward crew compartment before being removed altogether.|alt= ]]

During the [[Vietnam War]], B-52D [[tail gunner]]s were credited with shooting down two [[Mikoyan-Gurevich MiG-21|MiG-21 "Fishbeds"]]. On 18 December 1972 tail gunner [[Staff Sergeant]] Samuel O. Turner's B-52 had just completed a bomb run for Operation Linebacker II and was turning away when a [[Vietnam People's Air Force]] (VPAF) MiG-21 approached.{{sfn|McCarthy|2009|p=139}} The MiG and the B-52 locked onto each other. When the fighter drew within range, Turner fired his quad (four guns on one mounting) [[.50 caliber machine gun|.50 (12.7 mm) caliber machine gun]]s.{{sfn|McCarthy|2009|p=19}} The MiG exploded aft of the bomber,{{sfn|McCarthy|2009|p=139}} as confirmed by [[Master Sergeant]] Louis E. Le Blanc, the tail gunner in a nearby Stratofortress. Turner received a [[Silver Star]] for his actions.{{sfn|Futrell|Greenhalgh|Grubb|Hasselwander|1976}} His B-52, tail number 56-676, is preserved on display with air-to-air kill markings at [[Fairchild Air Force Base]] in [[Spokane]], Washington.{{sfn|McCarthy|2009|p=139}}

On 24 December 1972, during the same bombing campaign, the B-52 ''Diamond Lil'' was headed to bomb the [[rail yard|railroad yards]] of [[Thái Nguyên province|Thái Nguyên]] when tail gunner [[Airman First Class]] Albert E. Moore spotted a fast-approaching MiG-21.{{sfn|McCarthy|2009|p=141}} Moore opened fire with his quad .50 caliber guns at {{convert|4000|yd|m|abbr=on}}, and kept shooting until the fighter disappeared from his scope. [[Technical Sergeant]] Clarence W. Chute, a tail gunner aboard another Stratofortress, watched the MiG catch fire and fall away;{{sfn|McCarthy|2009|p=19}} this was not confirmed by the VPAF.<ref name="Toperczer #29 2001">Toperczer No. 29 2001.{{Full citation needed|date=March 2023}}</ref> ''Diamond Lil'' is preserved on display at the [[United States Air Force Academy]] in [[Colorado]].{{sfn|McCarthy|2009|p=141}} Moore was the last [[bomber]] gunner believed to have shot down an enemy aircraft with machine guns in [[aerial combat]].{{sfn|McCarthy|2009|p=19}}

The two B-52 tail gunner kills were not confirmed by VPAF, and they admitted to the loss of only three MiGs, all by F-4s.<ref name="Toperczer #29 2001" /> Vietnamese sources have attributed a third air-to-air victory to a B-52, a MiG-21 shot down on 16 April 1972.{{sfn|Toperczer|1998}} These victories make the B-52 the largest aircraft credited with air-to-air kills.{{refn|The following military aircraft are the only aircraft larger than the B-52 in some manner (parameter listed in parentheses may not be the only figure that exceeds the corresponding parameter of the B-52) and possess an air-to-air capability; none has a combat kill: [[Convair B-36 Peacemaker]] (wingspan), [[Convair YB-60]] (wingspan), [[Ilyushin Il-76]]D (payload).|group=Note}} The last Arc Light mission without fighter escort took place on 15 August 1973, as US military action in Southeast Asia was wound down.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=MB8tAAAAIBAJ&sjid=eqQEAAAAIBAJ&pg=4953,2976730&dq=vietnam&hl=en |title=Cambodia is a key to Vietnam peace |work=Rock Hill Herald |date=24 August 1973 |access-date=29 June 2021 |url-status=dead}}{{404<!-- (that's an error) -->|date=May 2026}}</ref>{{Better source needed|date=May 2026}}

===Post-Vietnam War service===
B-52Bs reached the end of their structural service life by the mid-1960s and all were retired by June 1966, followed by the last of the B-52Cs on 29 September 1971; except for [[NASA]]'s B-52B "[[Balls 8|008]]" which was eventually retired in 2004 at [[Edwards Air Force Base]], California.<ref>{{cite web |last=Creech |first=Gray |url=https://www.nasa.gov/vision/earth/improvingflight/B-52B_retires.html |title=End of an Era: NASA's Famous B-52B Retires |publisher=[[NASA]] |date=14 December 2004 |access-date=2 March 2023 |archive-date=28 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230228163803/https://www.nasa.gov/vision/earth/improvingflight/B-52B_retires.html |url-status=dead}}</ref> Another of the remaining B Models, "[[Wings Over the Rockies Air and Space Museum#B-52B Stratofortress s/n 52-005 (previously RB-52B)|52-005]]" is on display at the [[Wings Over the Rockies Air and Space Museum]] in [[Denver]], Colorado.<ref>{{cite web |title=B-52B Stratofortress |url=https://wingsmuseum.org/exhibits/boeing-b-52b-stratofortress/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090907040306/http://www.wingsmuseum.org/exhibit_stratofortress.asp |archive-date=7 September 2009 |website=Wings Over the Rockies Air & Space Museum |access-date=3 November 2009}}</ref>

[[File:B-52 with two D-21s.jpg|thumb|alt=Aerial view of B-52 fly above white clouds and the sea. It carries two triangular-shaped vehicles under the wings between the fuselage and inboard engines.|B-52H modified to carry two [[Lockheed D-21]]B drones]]
A few time-expired E models were retired in 1967 and 1968, but the bulk (82) were retired between May 1969 and March 1970. Most F models were also retired between 1967 and 1973, but 23 survived as trainers until late 1978. The fleet of D models served much longer;<ref name=pfusflt>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=mIRfAAAAIBAJ&sjid=4DAMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3852%2C7963535 |work=Lewiston Morning Tribune |location=Idaho |agency=(Los Angeles Times) |last=Seiler |first=Michael |title=Prepared for an unscheduled flight |date=29 January 1978 |page=3C}}</ref> 80 D models were extensively overhauled under the ''Pacer Plank'' program during the mid-1970s.{{sfn|Holder|1978}} Skinning on the lower wing and fuselage was replaced, and various structural components were renewed. The fleet of D models stayed largely intact until late 1978 when 37 not already upgraded Ds were retired.{{sfn|Willis|2005b|p=39}} The remainder were retired between 1982 and 1983.{{sfn|Willis|2005b|p=41}}

The remaining G and H models were used for nuclear standby ("alert") duty as part of the United States' [[nuclear triad]]; the combination of nuclear-armed land-based missiles, submarine-based missiles, and manned bombers. The B-1, intended to supplant the B-52, replaced only the older models and the supersonic FB-111.{{sfn|Anderton|1985|pp=98–99, 174}} In 1991, B-52s ceased continuous 24-hour SAC alert duty.<ref>{{cite web |last=Bailey |first=Carl E |url=https://www.dafhistory.af.mil/About-Us/Fact-Sheets/Display/Article/433570/325-weapons-squadron-acc/ |title=325 Weapons Squadron (ACC) |website=Air Force Historical Research Agency |publisher=United States Air Force |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100611011237/http://www.afhra.af.mil/factsheets/factsheet_print.asp?fsID=12123&page=1 |archive-date=11 June 2010}}</ref>

After Vietnam, the experience of operations in a hostile air defense environment was considered. Due to this, B-52s were modernized with new weapons, equipment, and both offensive and defensive avionics. This, and the use of low-level tactics, marked a major shift in the B-52's utility. The upgrades were:
* Supersonic short-range nuclear missiles: G and H models were modified to carry up to 20 SRAM missiles, replacing existing gravity bombs. Eight SRAMs were carried internally on a special rotary launcher and 12 SRAMs were mounted on two wing pylons. With SRAM, the B-52s could strike heavily defended targets without entering the terminal defenses.
* New countermeasures: Phase VI ECM modification, codenamed "Rivet Ace",{{r|RivetAce}} was the sixth major ECM program for the B-52. It improved the aircraft's self-protection capability in the dense Soviet air defense environment. The new equipment expanded signal coverage, improved threat warnings, provided new countermeasures techniques, and increased the quantity of expendables. The power requirements of Phase VI ECM also consumed most of the excess electrical capacity on the B-52G.
* B-52G and Hs were also modified with an electro-optical viewing system (EVS) that made low-level operations and terrain avoidance much easier and safer. EVS system contained a low light level television (LLTV) camera and a forward-looking infrared (FLIR) camera to display information needed for penetration at lower altitudes.
* Subsonic-cruise unarmed decoy: SCUD resembled the B-52 on the radar. As an active decoy, it carried ECM and other devices, and it had a range of several hundred miles. Although SCUD was never deployed operationally, the concept was developed, becoming known as the air-launched cruise missile (ALCM-A).

These modifications increased weight by nearly {{convert|24000|lb|kg}} and decreased operational range by 8–11%. This was considered acceptable for the increase in capabilities.<ref name=fasb52>{{cite web |url=https://fas.org/nuke/guide/usa/bomber/b-52_hist.htm |title=B-52 Stratofortress History |date=1 February 1998 |publisher=[[Federation of American Scientists]] |access-date=4 June 2016 |archive-date=8 July 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160708152300/http://fas.org/nuke/guide/usa/bomber/b-52_hist.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref>

After the fall of the Soviet Union, all B-52Gs remaining in service were destroyed in accordance with the terms of the [[START I|Strategic Arms Reduction Treaty]] (START). The [[309th Aerospace Maintenance and Regeneration Group|Aerospace Maintenance and Regeneration Center]] (AMRC) cut the 365 B-52s into pieces. Russia verified the completion destruction task via satellite and first-person inspection at the AMARC facility.{{sfn|Willis|2005b|pp=51–52}}

===Gulf War and later===
{{See also|Gulf War}}
[[File:B52sdestroyed.jpg|thumb|left|alt=Aerial view of B-52s and other aircraft slowly being scrapped in the desert.|Retired B-52s are stored at the [[309th Aerospace Maintenance and Regeneration Group|309th AMARG]] (formerly AMARC), a desert storage facility often called the "Boneyard" at [[Davis–Monthan Air Force Base]], near [[Tucson, Arizona|Tucson]], Arizona.{{sfn|Arana-Barradas|2001}}]]

B-52 strikes were an important part of [[Operation Desert Storm]]. Starting on 16 January 1991, a flight of B-52Gs flew from [[Barksdale Air Force Base]], Louisiana, refueled in the air en route, struck targets in Iraq, and returned home{{Spaced en dash}} a journey of 35 hours and {{convert|14000|mi|km}} round trip. It set a record for the longest-distance combat mission, breaking the record previously held by an RAF [[Avro Vulcan|Vulcan bomber]] in 1982; however, this was achieved using forward refueling.<ref name="Barksdale_Fact_Sheet"/>{{sfn|Willis|2005b|p=50}} Those seven B-52s flew the first combat sorties of Operation Desert Storm, firing 35 [[AGM-86|AGM-86C CALCM]] standoff missiles and successfully destroying 85–95 percent of their targets.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.flightglobal.com/news/articles/boeing-b-52-evolves-again-with-guided-weapons-launch-420874/ |title=Boeing B-52 evolves again with guided weapons launcher |work=FlightGlobal |date=15 January 2016}}</ref> B-52Gs operating from the [[King Abdulaziz International Airport|King Abdullah Air Base]] at Jeddah, Saudi Arabia, [[RAF Fairford]] in the United Kingdom, [[Morón Air Base]], Spain, and the island of [[Diego Garcia]] in the [[British Indian Ocean Territory]] flew bombing missions over Iraq, initially at low altitude. After the first three nights, the B-52s moved to high-altitude missions instead, which reduced their effectiveness and psychological impact compared to the low-altitude role initially played.{{sfn|Cordesman|Wagner|1996|p=451}}

The conventional strikes were carried out by three bombers, which dropped up to 153 of the {{cvt|750|lb|adj=on}} [[M117 bomb]] over an area of {{convert|1.5|by|1|mi|km|abbr=on}}. The bombings demoralized the defending Iraqi troops, many of whom surrendered in the wake of the strikes.{{sfn|Dick|Patterson|2006|p=225}} In 1999, the science and technology magazine ''[[Popular Mechanics]]'' described the B-52's role in the conflict: "The Buff's value was made clear during the Gulf War and Desert Fox. The B-52 turned out the lights in Baghdad."{{sfn|Garvey|1999}} During Operation Desert Storm, B-52s flew about 1,620 sorties and delivered 40% of the weapons dropped by coalition forces.<ref name="AF B52"/>

During the conflict, several claims of Iraqi air-to-air successes were made, including an Iraqi pilot, Khudai Hijab, who allegedly fired a [[Vympel R-27]]R missile from his [[MiG-29]] and damaged a B-52G on the opening night of the Gulf War.{{sfn|Lake|2004|p=48}} However, the USAF disputes this claim, stating the bomber was actually hit by friendly fire, an [[AGM-88 HARM|AGM-88]] High-speed, Anti-Radiation Missile (HARM) that homed on the fire-control radar of the B-52's tail gun; the jet was subsequently nicknamed ''In HARM's Way''.{{sfn|Lake|2004|pp=47–48}} Shortly following this incident, General [[George Lee Butler]] announced that the gunner position on B-52 crews would be eliminated, and the gun turrets permanently deactivated, commencing on 1 October 1991.{{sfn|Condor|1994|p=44}}

Since the mid-1990s, the B-52H has been the only variant remaining in military service;{{refn|A B-52B, [[Balls 8]], was in use by NASA, a civilian US government entity, until 17 December 2004.|group=Note}} it is currently stationed at:
* [[Minot Air Force Base]], [[North Dakota]]{{Spaced en dash}} [[5th Bomb Wing]]
* [[Barksdale Air Force Base]], [[Louisiana]]{{Spaced en dash}} [[2nd Bomb Wing]] (active Air Force) and [[307th Bomb Wing]] ([[Air Force Reserve Command]])
* One B-52H is assigned to [[Edwards Air Force Base]] and is used by [[Air Force Materiel Command]] at the USAF Flight Test Center.
* One additional B-52H has been used by NASA at Dryden Flight Research Center (now [[Armstrong Flight Research Center]]), California as part of the Heavy-lift Airborne Launch program.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nasa.gov/centers/dryden/history/pastprojects/B-52/index.html |title=Past Projects: B-52 Heavy-lift Airborne Launch Aircraft |publisher=NASA |access-date=12 August 2009}}</ref>

From 2 to 3 September 1996, two B-52Hs conducted a mission as part of [[Operation Desert Strike]]. The B-52s struck Baghdad power stations and communications facilities with 13 AGM-86C conventional air-launched cruise missiles (CALCM) during a 34-hour, {{cvt|16,000|mi|adj=on}} round trip mission from Andersen Air Force Base, Guam, the longest distance ever flown for a combat mission.{{sfn|Dick|Patterson|2006|p=222}}

[[File:B-52 Stratofortress Takeoff.jpg|thumb|alt=|A B-52H of the [[2nd Bomb Wing]] takes off from [[Andersen Air Force Base]], [[Guam]]]]
On 24 March 1999, when [[Operation Allied Force]] began, B-52 bombers bombarded Serb targets throughout the [[Federal Republic of Yugoslavia]], including during the [[Battle of Kosare]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Steele |first=Johnathan |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/1999/jul/17/balkans |title=Ghost village marks the battle that ended the war |work=The Guardian |date=17 July 1999}}</ref>

The B-52 contributed to [[War in Afghanistan (2001–present)|Operation Enduring Freedom]] in 2001 (Afghanistan/Southwest Asia), providing the ability to loiter high above the battlefield and provide Close Air Support (CAS) through the use of precision-guided munitions, a mission which previously would have been restricted to fighter and ground attack aircraft.{{sfn|Willis|2005b|pp=56–57}} In late 2001, ten B-52s dropped a third of the bomb tonnage in Afghanistan.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.strategypage.com/htmw/htairfo/20131010.aspx |title=Smart Bombs Keep B-52s in Business |website=StrategyPage.com |date=10 October 2013}}</ref> B-52s also played a role in [[2003 invasion of Iraq|Operation Iraqi Freedom]], which commenced on 20 March 2003 (Iraq/Southwest Asia). On the night of 21 March 2003, B-52Hs launched at least 100 AGM-86C CALCMs at targets within Iraq.{{sfn|Dick|Patterson|2006|p=242}}

===B-52 and maritime operations===
The B-52 can be employed in ocean surveillance, anti-ship and mine-laying operations. For example, a pair of B-52s, in two hours, can monitor {{convert|140000|mi2|km2|abbr=off|sp=us}} of the ocean surface. During the 2018 Baltops exercise, B-52s conducted mine-laying missions off the coast of Sweden, simulating a counter-amphibious invasion mission in the Baltic.<ref name=fasb52/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://theaviationist.com/2015/06/14/photos-show-b-52s-loaded-with-mines-for-simulated-massive-naval-mine-drop-outside-sweden/ |title=Photos show B-52s loaded with mines for simulated massive naval mine drop outside Sweden |first=David |last=Cenciotti |date=14 June 2015}}</ref>

In the 1970s, the US Navy worried that combined attacks from Soviet bombers, submarines, and warships could overwhelm its defenses and sink its aircraft carriers. After the [[Falklands War]], US planners feared the damage that could be created by {{convert|200|mi|nmi km|adj=on}}-range missiles carried by [[Tupolev Tu-22M]] "Backfire" bombers and {{convert|250|mi|nmi km|adj=on}}-range missiles carried by Soviet surface ships. New US Navy maritime strategy in the early 1980s called for the aggressive use of carriers and surface action groups against the Soviet navy. To help protect the carrier battle groups, some B-52Gs were modified to fire Harpoon anti-ship missiles. These bombers were based in Guam and Maine in the later 1970s to support both the Atlantic and Pacific fleets. In case of war, B-52s would coordinate with tanker support and surveillance aircraft. B-52Gs could strike Soviet Navy targets on the flanks of the US carrier battle groups, leaving them free to concentrate on offensive strikes against Soviet surface combatants. Mines laid by B-52s could establish minefields in significant enemy chokepoints (mainly the [[Kuril Islands]] and the [[GIUK gap]]). These minefields would force the Soviet fleet to disperse, making individual ships more vulnerable to Harpoon attacks.<ref>{{cite report |last1=Autry |first1=Dale |last2=Norton |first2=Donald G |title=The Increasing Maritime Role of the U.S. Air Force |publisher=Airforce University |date=May 1989 |id=AD-A217 654 |url=https://apps.dtic.mil/dtic/tr/fulltext/u2/a217654.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191225145919/https://apps.dtic.mil/dtic/tr/fulltext/u2/a217654.pdf |url-status=live |archive-date=25 December 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.airforcemag.com/MagazineArchive/Documents/2015/June%202015/0615ships.pdf |first=Rebecca |last=Grant |title=Airpower Against Ships |magazine=Airforce Magazine |date=June 2015 |pages=34–38}} {{Dead link|date=November 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

From the 1980s, B-52Hs were modified to use a wide range of cruise missiles, laser- and satellite-guided bombs, and unguided munitions. B-52 bomber crews honed sea-skimming flight profiles that would allow them to penetrate stiff enemy defenses and attack Soviet ships.<ref>{{cite report |first1=Donald G |last1=Cook |first2=Charles H |last2=Horne |first3=Walter W |last3=Manning |title=B-52 Maritime Operation: The Anti-Surface Warfare Mission (ASUW) |publisher=Airforce University |date=March 1987 |id=AD-A186 624 |url=https://apps.dtic.mil/dtic/tr/fulltext/u2/a186624.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191222014808/https://apps.dtic.mil/dtic/tr/fulltext/u2/a186624.pdf |url-status=live |archive-date=22 December 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://warisboring.com/these-madmen-flew-b-52-bombers-at-wave-top-heights/ |title=These Madmen Flew B-52 Bombers at Wave-Top Heights |website=War is Boring |date=22 July 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.vintagewings.ca/stories/lower-than-a-snakes-belly |title=Lower Than a Snake's Belly in a Wagon Rut Redux > Vintage Wings of Canada |website=VintageWings.ca |access-date=29 October 2023 |archive-date=20 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230820163558/https://www.vintagewings.ca/stories/lower-than-a-snakes-belly |url-status=live}}</ref>

Recent expansion and modernization of the [[People's Liberation Army Navy]] of China has caused the USAF to re-implement strategies for finding and attacking ships. The B-52 fleet has been certified to use the [[Naval mine#MK65 Quickstrike|Quickstrike]] family of naval mines using JDAM-ER guided wing kits. This weapon provides the ability to lay minefields over wide areas, in a single pass, with extreme accuracy, at a range of over {{convert|40|mi|nmi km}}. Besides this, with a view to enhancing B-52 maritime patrol and strike performance, an AN/ASQ-236 Dragon's Eye underwing pod, has also been certified for use by B-52H bombers. Dragon's Eye contains an advanced electronically scanned array radar that will allow B-52s to quickly scan vast Pacific Ocean areas. This radar will complement the Litening infrared targeting pod already used by B-52s for inspecting ships.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://warisboring.com/transforming-the-b-52-into-a-maritime-patrol-plane/ |title=Transforming the B-52 into a Maritime Patrol Plane |work=War Is Boring |date=27 June 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.pacaf.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/1592273/us-air-force-b-52s-train-with-us-navy-p-8s-in-east-china-sea/ |title=U.S. Air Force B-52s train with U.S. Navy P-8s in East China Sea |website=Pacific Air Forces |date=2 August 2018 |access-date=31 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250321121201/https://www.pacaf.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/1592273/us-air-force-b-52s-train-with-us-navy-p-8s-in-east-china-sea/ |archive-date=21 March 2025 |url-status=dead }}</ref> In 2019, Boeing selected the [[Raytheon]] to develop the new AN/APQ-188 AESA radar to replace its mechanically scanning [[AN/APQ-166]] attack radar; the APQ-188 is a variant of the [[AN/APG-79]] and also incorporates technology from the [[AN/APG-82]](V)1.<ref>{{cite news |first=Vivienne |last=Machi |url=https://www.defensedaily.com/raytheon-wins-contract-to-provide-new-aesa-radars-to-b-52-aircraft/air-force/ |title=Raytheon Wins Contract to Provide New AESA Radars to B-52 Aircraft |work=Defense Daily |date=11 July 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.raytheonintelligenceandspace.com/news/2020/02/12/new-eyes-old-friend |title=New eyes for an old friend |date=12 February 2020 |publisher=Raytheon}}</ref><ref>{{cite report |url=https://www.esd.whs.mil/Portals/54/Documents/FOID/Reading%20Room/Selected_Acquisition_Reports/FY_2023_SARS/B-52_RMP_MSAR_Dec_2023.pdf |title=Modernized Selected Acquisition Report (MSAR), B-52 Radar Modernization Program (RMP) (B-52 RMP) |date=31 December 2023 |publisher=U.S. Department of Defense}}</ref>

===21st century service===
[[File:Operation Inherent Resolve.jpg|thumb|B-52 Crew Chief SSgt Figueroa stenciling completed bombing sorties on B-52H (61–0010) during &nbsp;[[Operation Inherent Resolve]], in part of the [[Military intervention against ISIL]] at [[Al Udeid Air Base]], in Qatar]]

On 20 August 2003, a B-52 publicly landed in Russia for the first time, at [[Zhukovsky International Airport]] for the [[MAKS (air show)|MAKS]] air show. In the early 1990s a B-52 "flew into Russia" as part of a "military contact visit".<ref>{{Cite news |title=B-52 lands at Moscow air show |url=https://www.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/138678/b-52-lands-at-moscow-air-show/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428175938/https://www.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/138678/b-52-lands-at-moscow-air-show/ |archive-date=28 April 2023 |access-date=1 April 2026 |work=Air Force |language=en-US |url-status=live }}</ref>

In August 2007, a B-52H ferrying AGM-129 ACM cruise missiles from Minot Air Force Base to Barksdale Air Force Base for dismantling was [[2007 United States Air Force nuclear weapons incident|mistakenly loaded]] with six missiles with their nuclear warheads. The weapons did not leave USAF custody and were secured at Barksdale.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Warrick |first1=Joby |first2=Walter |last2=Pincus |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/story/2007/09/23/ST2007092300048.html |title=Missteps in the Bunker |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=23 September 2007}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Hoffman |first=Michael |url=https://www.indybay.org/newsitems/2007/10/20/18454937.php |title=Wing decertified, COs sacked for nuke mistake |work=Air Force Times |date=21 October 2007}}</ref>

Four of 18 B-52Hs from [[Barksdale Air Force Base]] were retired and were in the "boneyard" of 309th AMARG at [[Davis-Monthan Air Force Base]] as of 8 September 2008.<ref>{{cite web |last=Hopper |first=David |title=B-52 Stratofortress |url=http://www.af.mil/information/factsheets/factsheet.asp?id=838 |publisher=[[United States Air Force|Air Force]] |date=15 April 2011 |access-date=15 April 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121022014415/http://www.af.mil/information/factsheets/factsheet.asp?id=838 |archive-date=22 October 2012}}</ref>

[[File:B-52H 61-007 Ghost Rider.png|alt=The exterior of a B-52 cockpit.|thumb|B-52H "Ghost Rider" leaving the "bone yard"]]
In February 2015, (61-0007) ''Ghost Rider'' became the first stored B-52 to return to service after six years in storage at Davis-Monthan Air Force Base.<ref>{{Cite web |first=John |last=Parker |title='Ghost Rider' in the sky: B-52 departs Tinker in historic flight |work=Air Force |url=https://www.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/961002/ghost-rider-in-the-sky-b-52-departs-tinker-in-historic-flight |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170505020239/http://www.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/961002/ghost-rider-in-the-sky-b-52-departs-tinker-in-historic-flight/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=5 May 2017 |date=30 September 2016 |publisher=United States Air Force}}</ref>

In May 2019, a second aircraft (60-0034) was resurrected from long-term storage in Davis-Monthan. The B-52, nicknamed "Wise Guy", had been at AMARG since 2008. It flew to Barksdale Air Force Base on 13 May 2019. It was completed in four months by a team of 13–20 maintainers from the 307th Maintenance Squadron.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Losey |first=Stephen |date=17 May 2019 |title='Wise Guy' flies again: B-52 resurrected from Boneyard |url=https://www.airforcetimes.com/news/your-air-force/2019/05/16/wise-guy-flies-again-b-52-resurrected-from-boneyard/ |website=Air Force Times}}</ref>

[[File:B-52H 60-034 Wise Guy (Master Sgt. Ted Daigle, 307th Bomb Wing USAF).png|thumb|B-52 "Wise Guy" leaving the "bone yard"]]
B-52s are periodically refurbished at USAF maintenance depots such as [[Tinker Air Force Base]], [[Oklahoma]].<ref name="NSIAD-97-210BR">{{Cite report |url=https://www.gao.gov/assets/nsiad-97-210br.pdf |title=Information on the Cost-Effectiveness of B-1 and B-52 Support Options |date=September 1997 |publisher=United States [[General Accounting Office]] |id=GAO/NSIAD-97-210BR}}</ref>  Even while the USAF works on the new [[Long Range Strike Bomber]], it intends to keep the B-52H in service until 2050, which is 95 years after the B-52 first entered service (and will be about 88 years after the last B-52H was delivered to the US Air Force), an unprecedented length of service for any aircraft, civilian or military.<ref name="AF B52"/><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.airandspaceforces.com/article/0207force/|title=Strategic Force|last=Hebert|first=Adam J.|volume=90|issue=2|date=February 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241105061617/https://www.airandspaceforces.com/article/0207force/|archive-date=5 November 2024|url-status=live|magazine=[[Air & Space Forces Magazine]]|access-date=9 January 2008}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cnet.com/science/sixty-years-on-the-b-52-is-still-going-strong/ |title=Sixty years on, the B-52 is still going strong |work=CNET Military Tech News |date=15 April 2012}}</ref>{{refn|At least one B-52 aviator's father and grandfather also flew the bomber.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Three Generations of B-52 Airmen |date=25 October 2012 |website=Minot Air Force Base |url=https://www.minot.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/264580/three-generations-of-b-52-airmen}}</ref><ref name="ogden20110919">{{Cite news |last=Ogden |first=Eloise |date=19 September 2011 |title=Third generation B-52 flight officer |work=Minot Daily News |url=http://www.minotdailynews.com/page/content.detail/id/558723/Third-generation-B-52-flight-officer.html?nav=5013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111003001708/http://www.minotdailynews.com/page/content.detail/id/558723/Third-generation-B-52-flight-officer.html?nav=5013 |archive-date=3 October 2011}}</ref>|group=Note}}

The USAF continues to rely on the B-52 because it remains an effective and economical heavy bomber in the absence of sophisticated air defenses, particularly in the type of missions that have been conducted since the end of the Cold War against nations with limited defensive capabilities. The B-52 has also continued in service because there has been no reliable replacement.<ref>{{Cite news |title=After 60 Years, B-52s Still Dominate U.S. Fleet |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/12/06/us/b-52s-us-air-force-bombers.html |newspaper=The New York Times |date=5 December 2015 |issn=0362-4331 |first=Dave |last=Philipps}}</ref> The B-52 has the capacity to "loiter" for extended periods, and can deliver precision [[Standoff missile|standoff]] and [[direct fire]] munitions from a distance, in addition to direct bombing. It has been a valuable asset in supporting ground operations during conflicts such as [[Iraq War|Operation Iraqi Freedom]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Cortes |first=Lorenzo |url=http://www.encyclopedia.com/doc/1G1-102677802.html |title=B-52 Crew Credits Arsenal, Loiter Capability During Operation Iraqi Freedom |work=Defense Daily |date=9 May 2003}}</ref> The B-52 had the highest [[availability|mission capable rate]] of the three types of heavy bombers operated by the USAF in the 2000–2001 period. The B-1 averaged a 53.7% ready rate, the B-2 Spirit achieved 30.3%, and the B-52 averaged 80.5%.{{sfn|Arana-Barradas|2001}} The B-52's {{US$|72,000}} cost per hour of flight is more than the B-1B's {{US$|63,000}} cost per hour, but less than the B-2's {{US$|135,000}} per hour.<ref name="axe20120326">{{cite news |last=Axe |first=David |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/national/archive/2012/03/why-cant-the-air-force-build-an-affordable-plane/254998/ |title=Why Can't the Air Force Build an Affordable Plane? |work=The Atlantic |date=26 March 2012}}</ref>

The Long Range Strike Bomber program is intended to yield a stealthy successor for the B-52 and B-1 that would begin service in the 2020s; it is intended to produce 80 to 100 aircraft. Two competitors, Northrop Grumman and a joint team of Boeing and Lockheed Martin, submitted proposals in 2014;<ref name="Successor, Fortune">{{cite news |last1=Dillow |first1=Clay |title=This single Pentagon program is going to upend the combat jet industry |url=http://fortune.com/2015/03/05/long-range-strike-bomber/ |work=Fortune |date=5 March 2015 |quote=The program will produce 80 to 100 aircraft (at roughly {{US$|550}}{{nbsp}}million per copy), replacing the USAF's legacy fleet of B-52 and B-1 bombers with a stealthy, long-range, nuke-capable strike aircraft entering service around 2020.}}</ref> Northrop Grumman was awarded a contract in October 2015.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/10/28/business/northrop-grumman-wins-21-4-billion-pentagon-contract.html |title=Northrop Grumman Wins $21.4 Billion Pentagon Contract |date=28 October 2015 |work=The New York Times}}</ref>

On 12 November 2015, the B-52 began [[freedom of navigation]] operations in the [[South China Sea]] in response to [[Chinese expansionism|Chinese human-made islands]] in the region. Chinese forces, [[Territorial disputes in the South China Sea#United States|claiming jurisdiction]] within a 12-mile exclusion zone of the islands, ordered the bombers to leave the area, but they refused, not recognizing jurisdiction.<ref>{{cite web |title=US flies B-52 bombers near disputed islands claimed by China |url=https://thehill.com/policy/defense/259958-us-flies-b-52-bombers-near-islands-claimed-by-china/ |website=The Hill |first=Dustin |last=Weaver |date=12 November 2015}}</ref> On 10 January 2016, a B-52 overflew parts of South Korea escorted by South Korean F-15Ks and US F-16s in response to the supposed test of a hydrogen bomb by North Korea.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.cnn.com/2016/01/10/us/b-52-south-korea/ |title=U.S. B-52 bomber flies over S. Korea in solidarity after N. Korean nuclear claim |publisher=CNN |first=Holly |last=Yan |date=10 January 2016}}</ref>

On 9 April 2016, an undisclosed number of B-52s arrived at [[Al Udeid Air Base]] in Qatar as part of [[Operation Inherent Resolve]], part of the [[military intervention against ISIL]]. The B-52s took over heavy bombing after B-1 Lancers that had been conducting airstrikes rotated out of the region in January 2016.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.airforcetimes.com/news/your-air-force/2016/04/09/b-52s-arrive-in-qatar-to-join-bombing-campaign-against-isis/ |title=B-52s arrive in Qatar to join bombing campaign against ISIS |work=Air Force Times |date=9 April 2016}}</ref> In April 2016, B-52s arrived in Afghanistan to take part in the war in Afghanistan and began operations in July, proving its flexibility and precision carrying out close-air support missions.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.airforcetimes.com/story/military/2016/07/29/us-air-force-reports-sharp-climb-air-strikes-against-militants-afghanistan/87737638/ |title=U.S. Air Force reports sharp climb in air strikes against militants in Afghanistan |first=Oriana |last=Pawlyk |work=Air Force Times |date=29 July 2016}}</ref>

According to a statement by the US military, an undisclosed number of B-52s participated in the [[Battle of Khasham|US strikes]] on pro-government forces in eastern Syria on 7 February 2018.<ref name="harrigianstatement">{{cite press release |url=https://www.war.gov/News/Transcripts/Transcript/Article/1441080/department-of-defense-press-briefing-by-lieutenant-general-harrigian-via-teleco/ |title=News Transcript: Department of Defense Press Briefing by Lieutenant General Harrigian via teleconference from Al Udeid Airbase, Qatar |publisher=U.S. Department of Defense |date=13 February 2018 |access-date=7 December 2025 |archive-date=20 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251120234033/https://www.war.gov/News/Transcripts/Transcript/Article/1441080/department-of-defense-press-briefing-by-lieutenant-general-harrigian-via-teleco/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> A number of B-52s were deployed in airstrikes against the Taliban during the [[2021 Taliban offensive]].<ref>{{cite news |title=US B-52 Bombers, Gunships Sent into Action in Afghanistan in Attempt to Stop Taliban Advance on Key Cities |website=Military.com |via=[[The Times]] |date=8 August 2021 |url=https://www.military.com/daily-news/2021/08/08/us-b-52-bombers-gunships-sent-action-afghanistan-attempt-stop-taliban-advance-key-cities.html}}</ref> In 2022, the US Air Force used a B-52 as a platform to test a [[Hypersonic Air-breathing Weapon Concept]] (HAWC) missile.<ref>{{cite news |title=US tested hypersonic missile in mid-March but kept it quiet to avoid escalating tensions with Russia |first=Oren |last=Liebermann |date=4 April 2022 |publisher=CNN |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/04/04/politics/us-hypersonic-missile-test/index.html}}</ref> In late October 2022, [[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] reported that the USAF intended to deploy six B-52s at [[RAAF Base Tindal|RAAF Tindal]] in Australia in the near future, which would include building facilities to handle the aircraft.<ref>{{Cite news |date=30 October 2022 |title=A 'strong message to our adversaries': US prepares to deploy B-52 bombers to Top End |publisher=ABC News |location=Australia |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-10-31/china-tensions-taiwan-us-military-deploy-bombers-to-australia/101585380}}</ref>

[[File:B-52H Stratofortress with AGM-158 JASSM, Operation Epic Fury.jpg|thumb|B-52H Stratofortress with [[AGM-158 JASSM]] cruise missiles during the [[2026 Iran war]], 4 March 2026]]
On 3 November 2024, CENTCOM confirmed an undisclosed number of B-52s from Minot Air Force Base's 5th Bomb Wing arrived in the Middle East.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sorace |first=Stephen |date=3 November 2024 |title=US bombers arrive in Middle East as concerns of Iranian attack on Israel mount |url=https://www.foxnews.com/world/us-bombers-arrive-middle-east-concerns-iranian-attack-israel-mount |access-date=4 November 2024 |publisher=Fox News}}</ref> In December 2024, B-52s, alongside undisclosed numbers of [[McDonnell Douglas F-15E Strike Eagle|F-15E]] fighters and [[Fairchild Republic A-10 Thunderbolt II|A-10]] attack aircraft, participated in airstrikes against over 75 Islamic State targets within Syria, following the [[Fall of the Assad regime|ousting of the al-Assad government]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Allison |first=George |date=8 December 2024 |title=U.S. launches mass airstrikes against targets in Syria |url=https://ukdefencejournal.org.uk/u-s-launches-mass-airstrikes-against-targets-in-syria/ |access-date=9 December 2024 |language=en-GB}}</ref>

On 3 March 2026, CENTCOM announced that a B-52 bomber force was deployed during the [[2026 Iran war]], and was used to strike ballistic missile sites along with command & control posts within Iran.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Update from CENTCOM Commander on Operation Epic Fury: |url=https://x.com/CENTCOM/status/2028983418801803741 |url-status=live}}</ref> At the end of the month B-52 bombers began flying over Iranian territory after the level of air superiority achieved was deemed adequate.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Panella |first1=Chris |title=The US military says its 70-year-old B-52 bombers are now flying overland missions as air superiority expands over Iran |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/us-air-superiority-iran-b52-overland-flights-general-2026-3 |access-date=1 April 2026 |work=Business Insider}}</ref>

