The B-52 went through several design changes and variants over its 10 years of production.{{sfn|Knaack|1988}}

'''XB-52'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=Two prototype aircraft with limited operational equipment, used for aerodynamic and handling tests.}}

'''YB-52'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=One XB-52 modified with some operational equipment and re-designated.}}

'''B-52A'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=Only three of the first production version, the B-52A, were built, all loaned to Boeing for flight testing.{{sfn|Donald|1997}} The first production B-52A differed from prototypes in having a redesigned forward fuselage. The bubble canopy and tandem seating was replaced by a side-by-side arrangement and a {{convert|21|in|cm|adj=on}} nose extension accommodated more [[avionics]] and a new sixth crew member.{{refn|The electronic warfare officer sat in the rear of the upper flight deck facing the rear.{{sfn|Dorr|1990|p=26}} |group=Note}} In the rear fuselage, a tail turret with four {{convert|0.50|in|mm|1|adj=on}} machine guns with a fire-control system, and a [[water injection (engines)|water injection]] system to augment engine power with a {{cvt|360|USgal|impgal L|adj=on}} water tank were added. The aircraft also carried a {{convert|1000|USgal|impgal L|adj=on}} external fuel tank under each wing. The tanks damped wing flutter and kept wingtips close to the ground for ease of maintenance.{{sfn|Dorr|1990|p=26}}}}

'''NB-52A'''
[[File:Boeing NB-52A carrying X-15.jpg|thumb|alt=|NB-52A carrying an X-15]]
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=The last B-52A (serial 52-003) was modified and redesignated NB-52A in 1959 to carry the [[North American X-15]]. A pylon was fitted under the right wing between the fuselage and the inboard engines with a {{convert|6|x|8|ft|m|adj=on}} section removed from the right wing [[Flap (aircraft)|flap]] to fit the X-15 tail. Liquid oxygen and hydrogen peroxide tanks were installed in the bomb bays to fuel the X-15 before launch. Its first flight with the X-15 was on 19 March 1959, with the first launch on 8 June 1959. The NB-52A, nicknamed "The High and Mighty One" carried the X-15 on 93 of the program's 199 flights.{{sfn|Willis|2005a|p=70}}}}

'''B-52B'''
[[File:Nasa-b52-040721-wp-cr.jpg|thumb|alt=|NASA's NB-52B ''[[Balls 8]]'' (lower) and its replacement B-52H on the flight line at [[Edwards Air Force Base]] in 2004]]
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=The B-52B was the first version to enter service with the USAF on 29 June 1955 with the [[93rd Bombardment Wing]] at Castle Air Force Base, California.{{sfn|Dorr|1990|p=26}} This version included minor changes to engines and avionics, enabling an extra {{convert|12000|lb-f}} of thrust using water injection.{{sfn|Boyne|1982}} Temporary grounding of the aircraft after a crash in February 1956 and again the following July caused training delays, and at mid-year, there were still no combat-ready B-52 crews.{{sfn|Lake|2003a|p=119}}

Seven B-52Bs were brought to B-52C standard under ''Project Sunflower''.{{sfn|Willis|2005a|p=59}}}}

'''XR-16A/RB-52B'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=XR-16A was the initial designation allocated to the reconnaissance variant of the B-52B, but not used. The aircraft were designated RB-52Bs instead.{{sfn|Andrade|1979|p=55}}

Of the 50 B-52Bs built, 27 were capable of carrying a reconnaissance pod as RB-52Bs (the crew was increased to eight in these aircraft).{{sfn|Donald|1997}} The {{convert|300|lb|kg|adj=on}} pod contained radio receivers, a combination of K-36, K-38, and T-11 cameras, and two operators on downward-firing [[ejection seat]]s. The pod required only four hours to install.{{sfn|Lake|2003a|p=119}}}}

'''NB-52B'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=The NB-52B was B-52B number 52-8 converted to an X-15 launch platform. It subsequently flew as "[[Balls 8]]" in support of [[NASA]] research from 1966 to 17 December 2004, making it the oldest flying B-52B.<ref name=Balls8>{{cite web |url=https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/171366main_FS-005-DFRC.pdf |title=B-52B "Mothership" Launch Aircraft |publisher=NASA |access-date=29 October 2023}}</ref> It was replaced by a modified B-52H.{{sfn|Skipper|2024|p=53}}}}

'''B-52C/RB-52C'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=The B-52C's fuel capacity (and range) was increased to {{convert|41700|USgal|impgal L}} by adding larger {{convert|3000|USgal|impgal L|adj=on}} underwing fuel tanks. The gross weight was increased by {{convert|30000|lb|kg}} to {{convert|450000|lb|kg}}. A new fire control system, the MD-9, was introduced on this model.{{sfn|Condor|1994|p=37}} The belly of the aircraft was painted with [[anti-flash white]] paint, which was intended to reflect the thermal radiation of a nuclear detonation.{{sfn|Lake|2003a|p=121}}

The RB-52C was the designation initially given to B-52Cs fitted for reconnaissance duties in a similar manner to RB-52Bs. As all 35 B-52Cs could be fitted with the reconnaissance pod, the RB-52C designation was little used and was quickly abandoned.{{sfn|Lake|2003a|p=121}}}}

'''B-52D'''
[[File:B-52D dropping bombs.jpg|thumb|alt=B-52D|B-52D dropping 500-lb bombs]]
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=The B-52D was a dedicated long-range bomber without a reconnaissance option. The ''Big Belly'' modifications allowed the B-52D to carry heavy loads of conventional bombs for [[carpet bombing]] over Vietnam,{{sfn|Boyne|1982}} while the ''Rivet Rambler'' modification added the Phase V [[Electronic Counter Measures|ECM]] systems, which was better than the systems used on most later B-52s. Because of these upgrades and its long range capabilities, the D model was used more extensively in Vietnam than any other model.{{sfn|Condor|1994|p=37}} Aircraft assigned to Vietnam were painted in a camouflage color scheme with black bellies to defeat searchlights.{{sfn|Lake|2003b|pp=100–101}}}}

'''B-52E'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=The B-52E received updated avionics and bombing navigational system, which was eventually debugged and included in the following models.{{sfn|Boyne|1982}}}}

'''JB-52E'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=One aircraft leased by General Electric to test TF39 and CF6 engines.{{sfn|Boyne|1982}}}}

[[File:Boeing NB-52E during the Air Force Flight Dynamics Laboratory-Boeing Control Configured Vehicles program 061128-F-1234S-001.jpg|thumb|Boeing NB-52E during the Air Force Flight Dynamics Laboratory-Boeing Control Configured Vehicles program 061128-F-1234S-001]]
'''NB-52E'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=One aircraft (AF Serial No. 56-632) was modified as a testbed for various B-52 systems. Redesignated NB-52E, the aircraft was fitted with horizontal and vertical forward stabilizer fins near the cockpit and a Load Alleviation and Mode Stabilization system which reduced airframe fatigue from wind gusts during low-level flight. 
In one test, the aircraft flew {{convert|10|kn|mph km/h}} faster than the [[VNE|never exceed speed]] without damage because the forward stabilizer fins eliminated 30% of vertical and 50% of horizontal vibrations caused by wind gusts.{{sfnm|1a1=Phillips|1y=1998|1p=132|2a1=Knaack|2y=1988|2p=263}}<ref>{{cite web |first=Joe |last=Baugher |url=http://www.joebaugher.com/usaf_bombers/b52_11.html |title=Boeing B-52E Stratofortress |date=1 July 2000 |website=JoeBaugher.com |access-date=10 November 2017 |archive-date=7 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230407003952/http://www.joebaugher.com/usaf_bombers/b52_11.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>}}

'''B-52F'''
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=This aircraft was given J57-P-43W engines with a larger capacity water injection system to provide greater thrust than previous models.{{sfn|Boyne|1982}} This model had problems with fuel leaks which were eventually solved by several service modifications: ''Blue Band'', ''Hard Shell'', and ''QuickClip''.{{sfn|Knaack|1988|pp=266–267}}}}

{{Anchor|B-52G}}'''B-52G'''
[[File:B52LAFB.JPG|thumb|B-52G on static display at [[Langley Air Force Base]] in [[Hampton, Virginia|Hampton]], Virginia|alt=]]
[[File:B52_Griffiss.jpg|thumb|A static B-52G that resides at [[Griffiss International Airport]] (formerly Griffiss Air Force Base). Also pictured is an [[AGM-86 ALCM]] cruise missile.|alt=]]
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=The B-52G was proposed to extend the B-52's service life during delays in the [[B-58 Hustler]] program. At first, a radical redesign was envisioned with a completely new wing and [[Pratt & Whitney J75]] engines. This was rejected to avoid slowdowns in production, although a large number of changes were implemented.{{sfn|Boyne|1982}} The most significant of these was a new "wet" wing with integral fuel tanks, increasing gross aircraft weight by {{convert|38000|lb|kg}}. In addition, a pair of 700&nbsp;US&nbsp;gallon (2,650&nbsp;L) external fuel tanks were fitted under the wings on wet hardpoints.{{sfn|Tagg|2004|p=86}} The traditional [[aileron]]s were also eliminated, and the [[spoiler (aeronautics)|spoilers]] now provided all roll control (roll control had always been primarily with spoilers due to the danger of wing twist under aileron deflection, but older models had small "feeler" ailerons fitted to provide feedback to the controls). The tail fin was shortened by {{convert|8|ft|m}}, water injection system capacity was increased to 1,200&nbsp;US&nbsp;gallons (4,540&nbsp;L), and the nose [[radome]] was enlarged.{{sfn|Boyne|2001|p=221}} The tail gunner was relocated to the forward fuselage, aiming via a radar scope, and was now provided with an ejection seat.{{sfn|Tagg|2004|p=86}} Dubbed the "Battle Station" concept, the offensive crew (pilot and copilot on the upper deck and the two bombing navigation system operators on the lower deck) faced forward, while the defensive crew (tail gunner and ECM operator) on the upper deck faced aft.{{sfn|Condor|1994|p=37}} The B-52G entered service on 13 February 1959 (a day earlier, the last B-36 was retired, making SAC an all-jet bomber force). 193 B-52Gs were produced, making this the most produced B-52 variant. Most B-52Gs were destroyed in compliance with the 1992 ''[[START I|Strategic Arms Reduction Treaty]]''; the last B-52G, number 58-224, was dismantled under [[New START]] treaty requirements in December 2013.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Final B-52G eliminated under New START |work=Air Force |date=22 December 2013 |url=https://www.af.mil/News/Article-Display/Article/467809/final-b-52g-eliminated-under-new-start/ |access-date=3 March 2023 |publisher=United States Air Force}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.militarytimes.com/article/20131228/NEWS04/312280002/Last-B-52G-eliminated-under-nuclear-arms-treaty |title=Last B-52G eliminated under nuclear arms treaty |work=Military Times |date=28 December 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140101033332/http://www.militarytimes.com/article/20131228/NEWS04/312280002/Last-B-52G-eliminated-under-nuclear-arms-treaty |archive-date=1 January 2014}}</ref> A few examples remain on display for museums.<ref>{{cite news |title=A roaring farewell at Wurtsmith air base |url=http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/24332681.html?dids=24332681:24332681&FMT=CITE&FMTS=CITE:FT&type=current&date=Dec+04%2C+1992&author=Chicago+Tribune+wires.&pub=Chicago+Tribune+(pre-1997+Fulltext)&desc=A+roaring+farewell+at+Wurtsmith+air+base&pqatl=google |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |date=4 December 1992 |archive-date=25 July 2012 |access-date=28 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120725055948/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/chicagotribune/access/24332681.html?dids=24332681:24332681&FMT=CITE&FMTS=CITE:FT&type=current&date=Dec+04%2C+1992&author=Chicago+Tribune+wires.&pub=Chicago+Tribune+(pre-1997+Fulltext)&desc=A+roaring+farewell+at+Wurtsmith+air+base&pqatl=google |url-status=dead }}</ref>}}

{{Anchor|B-52H}}'''B-52H'''
[[File:A Boeing B-52H Stratofortress in flight over the Persian Gulf (190521-F-XN348-9173).jpg|thumb|A Boeing B-52H Stratofortress in flight over the Persian Gulf]]
{{Block indent |left=1 |text=The B-52H had the same crew and structural changes as the B-52G. The most significant upgrade was the switch to [[Pratt & Whitney JT3D|TF33-P-3]] turbofan engines, which, despite the initial reliability problems (corrected by 1964 under the ''Hot Fan'' program), offered considerably better performance and fuel economy than the J57 [[turbojet]]s.{{sfn|Condor|1994|p=37}}{{sfn|Boyne|2001|p=221}} The ECM and avionics were updated, a new fire control system was fitted, and the rear defensive armament was changed from machine guns to a 20{{nbsp}}mm [[M61 Vulcan]] cannon,{{sfn|Tagg|2004|p=86}} which were then removed between 1991 and 1994.{{citation needed|date=July 2024}} The final 18 aircraft were manufactured with provision for the [[ADR-8]] countermeasures rocket, which was later retrofitted to the remainder of the B-52G and B-52H fleet.{{sfn|Dorr|Peacock|2000|p=52}} A provision was made for four [[GAM-87 Skybolt|GAM-87 Skybolt ballistic missile]]s. The aircraft's first flight occurred on 10 July 1960, and it entered service on 9 May 1961. This is the only variant currently in use.<ref name="AF B52"/> A total of 102 B-52Hs were built. The last production aircraft, B-52H AF Serial No. 61-40, left the factory on 26 October 1962.{{sfn|Dorr|1990|p=27}}}}

{{Anchor|B-52J}}'''B-52J'''
{{Block indent|left=1|text=Upgrade of B-52H with [[Rolls-Royce BR700|Rolls-Royce F130]] engines, the AN/APQ-188 AESA radar (a variant of the [[AN/APG-79]] radar), and new communications and navigation equipment. To be operational before the end of the 2020s.<ref name="Tirpak 2023"/> It will improve fuel efficiency by 30 percent, state-of-the-art radar systems, engine pylons, digital engine controls, and other upgrades. The planned Initial Operational Capability for the new radar is 2027.<ref>{{cite web |last=Balestrieri |first=Steve |url=https://www.19fortyfive.com/2026/09/the-youngest-b-52-was-built-in-1962-and-the-us-air-force-plans-to-fly-it-until-2050-88-years-in-service-in-early-march-2026-b-52s-struck-ballistic-missile-sites-inside-iran-for-the-first-t/ |title=The Youngest B-52 Was Built in 1962 and the US Air Force Plans to Fly It Until 2050 — 88 Years in Service. In Early March 2026, B-52s Struck Ballistic Missile Sites Inside Iran for the First Time. |website=19FortyFive |date=2026-09-15 |access-date=2026-09-15}}</ref>}}

